    <node id="FAMILY MEMBERS">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Family members are the patient's blood relatives on both sides of the family whose health histories are collected for the family history. The care team asks about their diseases and genetic testing, as well as health issues such as heart disease, cancer, and diabetes, which are all relevant to understanding the patient's familial risk. Beyond their role in medical history, family members also provide practical and emotional support, including transportation, meals, emotional encouragement, and help taking notes during appointments.
Relationships:
  - Source: FAMILY MEMBERS
  - Target: PATIENT
  - Description: The patient is asked to talk to family members on both sides of the family about their health history.
  - Source: PATIENT
  - Target: FAMILY MEMBERS
  - Description: Family members can provide transportation, meals, emotional support, and note-taking for the patient
  - Source: CARE TEAM
  - Target: FAMILY MEMBERS
  - Description: The care team asks about the health history of family members who are blood relatives.
  - Source: FAMILY MEMBERS
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: Family members provide health history for the family history
  - Source: DOCTOR
  - Target: FAMILY MEMBERS
  - Description: The doctor asks about the health history of family members who are blood relatives
  - Source: FAMILY MEMBERS
  - Target: GENETIC TESTING
  - Description: Family members may have undergone genetic testing, and this information is an important part of their health history. The patient should be aware of whether any relatives have had genetic testing, as it can provide valuable context for understanding inherited risks and guiding medical decisions.
  - Source: FAMILY MEMBERS
  - Target: BLOOD RELATIVES
  - Description: Blood relatives are the family members whose health history should be gathered for family history</data>
