    <node id="NEUROSURGEON">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">A neurosurgeon is a physician and surgical specialist who performs surgical procedures for brain tumors, including both resections and stereotactic biopsies. They perform brain biopsy procedures—encompassing both stereotactic and open biopsies—in an operating room. During these procedures, the neurosurgeon makes openings in the skull, uses surgical instruments and navigation systems to accurately target the tumor, and receives lab results during the operation to determine how much tumor to remove. This role combines expertise in open surgical techniques with minimally invasive stereotactic approaches, allowing for precise diagnosis and treatment of brain lesions.
Relationships:
  - Source: NEUROSURGEON
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The neurosurgeon is the surgical member of the care team who performs resections and biopsies.
  - Source: NEUROSURGEON
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: The neurosurgeon is a specialist who performs surgical biopsy procedures, including stereotactic biopsy and resection, to obtain tissue samples for diagnostic evaluation. These biopsy procedures are conducted in the operating room, ensuring a controlled and sterile environment for the patient.
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: NEUROSURGEON
  - Description: The neurosurgeon performs surgery in the operating room to remove tumor tissue.
  - Source: NEUROSURGEON
  - Target: TREATMENT TEAM
  - Description: The neurosurgeon is part of the medical team that uses diagnostic information to plan treatment.
  - Source: NEUROSURGEON
  - Target: NEUROPATHOLOGIST
  - Description: The neuropathologist sends analysis results back to the neurosurgeon.
  - Source: OPEN BIOPSY
  - Target: NEUROSURGEON
  - Description: The neurosurgeon performs an open biopsy by opening the scalp and skull to remove tumor pieces.
  - Source: NEUROSURGEON
  - Target: RESECTION
  - Description: The neurosurgeon attempts to remove the entire tumor during a resection.
  - Source: RESECTION
  - Target: NEUROSURGEON
  - Description: The neurosurgeon performs tumor resection, and open biopsy is described as being similar to a resection.
  - Source: HISTOLOGY
  - Target: NEUROSURGEON
  - Description: Histology results help the neurosurgeon decide how much of the tumor to remove.
  - Source: LAB TESTING
  - Target: NEUROSURGEON
  - Description: Lab testing results are sent back to the neurosurgeon.
  - Source: NEUROSURGEON
  - Target: STEREOTACTIC BIOPSY
  - Description: The neurosurgeon performs a stereotactic biopsy in an operating room.
  - Source: STEREOTACTIC BIOPSY
  - Target: NEUROSURGEON
  - Description: The neurosurgeon performs a stereotactic biopsy using a frame, markers, and a navigation system.
  - Source: MOLECULAR TESTING
  - Target: NEUROSURGEON
  - Description: Molecular testing results help guide the neurosurgeon and treatment team in managing the glioma.
  - Source: PROVIDER
  - Target: NEUROSURGEON
  - Description: A provider may schedule a standard biopsy, and the neurosurgeon performs the surgical procedure.
  - Source: CRANIOTOMY
  - Target: NEUROSURGEON
  - Description: The neurosurgeon makes a craniotomy, or opening in the skull, during open biopsy and similar procedures.</data>
