    <node id="PHYSICAL EXAM">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">A physical exam is a clinical evaluation conducted by a health care provider to assess the body for signs of disease or abnormality. It is a hands-on examination that typically checks vital signs—such as blood pressure, heart rate, and temperature—along with height, weight, and overall body condition. The provider systematically examines the heart, lungs, organs, lymph nodes, skin, eyes, ears, nose, throat, head, and neck. In some cases, the exam may include palpation of the oral cavity or the use of a mirror or fiberoptic scope to visualize hard-to-see areas. Depending on the clinical context, a physical exam may also involve checking the breasts, spleen, or liver.

This examination serves as a general health test and is often integrated into specific medical workflows. For example, it is part of the diagnostic evaluation for basal cell skin cancer and is listed as a testing component for ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) and treatment planning. In oncology, it is performed by specialists such as hematologists/oncologists, who may also assess the spleen and liver. For patients who have undergone a total thyroidectomy, a physical exam is part of short-term follow-up care. It is also a component of surveillance for stage 2 and 3 colon cancer, where it helps detect possible signs of recurrence; suspicious findings may prompt further imaging or blood tests. Additionally, after chemoradiation for certain cancers, the physical exam is often combined with digital rectal examination and anoscopy. In all settings, the physical exam provides a foundational, non-invasive method for identifying abnormalities, guiding further diagnostic steps, and monitoring patient health over time.
Relationships:
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: COMPLETE HEAD AND NECK EXAM
  - Description: A physical exam will include the head and neck exam
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The care team performs a physical exam to evaluate the patient
  - Source: IMAGING
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: A physical exam that is suspicious for recurrence leads to imaging tests to confirm whether cancer has returned.
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: PROSTATE CANCER
  - Description: A physical exam checks for signs of disease, including enlarged lymph nodes that may indicate cancer spread
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: Physical exams are part of surveillance for stage 2 and 3 colon cancer.
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: DIAGNOSIS
  - Description: Physical exam is an integral component of the diagnosis process. It is also a key element of the broader diagnostic assessment, providing essential clinical information used to evaluate a patient's condition. Together, the physical exam and diagnosis are closely connected, with the physical exam serving as a foundational step in forming an accurate diagnosis.
  - Source: RECURRENCE
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: A physical exam may first raise suspicion of recurrence because of symptoms or abnormal findings.
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: GENERAL HEALTH TESTS
  - Description: General health tests include a physical exam
  - Source: GENERAL HEALTH TESTS
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: GENERAL HEALTH TESTS encompass a range of routine medical evaluations, and PHYSICAL EXAM is one of the key components included within them. In other words, a physical exam is one of the general health tests performed to assess overall health.
  - Source: BLOOD TEST
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: If a physical exam is suspicious for recurrence, blood tests may be ordered to check kidney and liver function.
  - Source: TOTAL THYROIDECTOMY
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: Physical exam is part of short-term follow-up after total thyroidectomy
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: HEALTH CARE PROVIDER
  - Description: A health care provider may perform a physical exam
  - Source: HEALTH CARE PROVIDER
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: A health care provider is responsible for performing the physical exam, during which vital signs are also checked. The physical exam is carried out by the health care provider as part of the assessment process.
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU
  - Description: Physical exam is listed as part of testing for DCIS.
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: The physical exam is performed together with anoscopy.
  - Source: FOLLOW-UP AND SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: Follow-up and surveillance includes a physical exam with digital rectal examination and anoscopy.
  - Source: CLINICAL STAGING
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: Clinical staging is based on a physical exam
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: BREAST EXAM
  - Description: A physical exam includes a breast exam
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: DIGITAL RECTAL EXAMINATION
  - Description: The physical exam is performed together with digital rectal examination.
  - Source: FOLLOW-UP TESTING AND SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: Physical exam is part of short-term follow-up after total thyroidectomy
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: HEMATOLOGIST/ONCOLOGIST
  - Description: The hematologist/oncologist performs the physical exam.
  - Source: GENERAL HEALTH HISTORY
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: A physical exam is part of the general health history and physical exam assessment
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: COMPLETE SKIN EXAM
  - Description: A complete skin exam is part of the physical exam
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY AND PHYSICAL EXAM (H&amp;P)
  - Description: The H&amp;P includes the physical exam component.
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: CLINICAL BREAST EXAM
  - Description: The clinical breast exam is a specific type of physical exam focused on the breast.</data>
