    <node id="CANCER TREATMENT">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">CANCER TREATMENT is a comprehensive term encompassing the general category of therapy for cancer, as well as the overall process of treating specific cancers, such as ovarian cancer. It includes surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, systemic therapy, and other therapies, including clinical trials. The treatment plan is developed by the care team after the cancer has been staged and graded, and it is based on tumor location and cancer stage, with strategies often planned using test results. Cancer treatment is directed at the cancer itself, but it can have significant side effects: it can reactivate hepatitis viruses, affect fertility, and may require pregnancy prevention. Additionally, smoking or vaping can make cancer treatment less effective; therefore, treatment is more effective if the patient stops smoking or vaping. These considerations apply across the various forms of cancer therapy described.
Relationships:
  - Source: TREATMENT
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Cancer treatment is a specific type of treatment.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Biopsy results are used to determine cancer stage treatment plan.
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Surgery and cancer treatment are closely interconnected, with surgery serving as a major therapeutic approach in oncology. Surgery is a form of cancer treatment that is used to remove cancerous tissue from the body. It is also one component of a broader cancer treatment plan, which may include other modalities such as radiation therapy, chemotherapy, or immunotherapy. In the context of head and neck cancers specifically, surgery is considered a complex form of cancer treatment due to the intricate anatomy of the region and the need for precise removal of tumors while preserving critical functions like breathing, swallowing, and speech. Additionally, discussions of surgery as a cancer treatment include attention to surgery side effects in cancer care, acknowledging that the procedure can have physical and functional impacts that must be managed before, during, and after treatment. Thus, surgery is both a distinct, direct method for excising cancer and an integral part of multidisciplinary cancer care, with its own risks and benefits that are weighed carefully by the medical team.
  - Source: CARE TEAM
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: The care team plans treatment and needs to know the stage and grade of the cancer.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a form of cancer treatment.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment and one component of the broader category of cancer treatment. It is used to combat cancer but can have side effects, notably affecting fertility. Thus, while chemotherapy is a key part of cancer treatment, it is not the entirety of it, and its impact on fertility is an important consideration for patients.
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Radiation therapy is a type of cancer treatment.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: IMAGING TESTS
  - Description: Imaging results are used to determine cancer stage treatment plan.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: OVARIAN CANCER
  - Description: The guide describes treatment recommendations for ovarian cancer as a cancer treatment process.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: DIAGNOSIS
  - Description: Emotional support is needed during diagnosis and treatment.
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Head and neck cancer and its treatment can affect the jaw.
  - Source: SYSTEMIC THERAPY
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Systemic therapy is a type of cancer treatment using medicines that travel through the bloodstream.
  - Source: BLADDER CANCER
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Bladder cancer treatment is planned based on staging and grading.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Clinical trials study the safety and effectiveness of investigational cancer treatments. Clinical trials may be included as options within a cancer treatment plan.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: TREATMENT TEAM
  - Description: The treatment team provides and coordinates cancer care, including distress screening as part of cancer care.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: TREATMENT PLANNING
  - Description: Treatment is planned before cancer treatment begins.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: GENETIC TESTING
  - Description: Genetic testing results may guide cancer treatment.
  - Source: STAGING
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Staging helps the care team select the best therapy.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history helps determine which treatment is best.
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Smoking can limit how well cancer treatment works.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: FERTILITY PRESERVATION
  - Description: Fertility preservation is an important consideration for individuals who are about to begin cancer treatment. It should be considered and planned before starting cancer treatment, as cancer therapies such as chemotherapy, radiation, or surgery may affect fertility. Therefore, fertility preservation should be addressed prior to the initiation of cancer treatment to allow for the best possible options and outcomes. In summary, both consideration and planning for fertility preservation should occur before starting cancer treatment.
  - Source: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Quitting smoking can make cancer treatment work better.
  - Source: SMOKEFREE.GOV
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Stopping smoking or vaping is essential to maximize the chance of cure and minimize side effects from cancer treatment; SmokeFree.gov is an online resource to help quit.
  - Source: VAPING
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Vaping can limit how well cancer treatment works.
  - Source: TESTING FOR OVARIAN CANCER
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Testing provides information that informs the cancer treatment plan.
  - Source: BETOBACCOFREE.GOV
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: BeTobaccoFree.gov is an online resource to help quit tobacco use, which improves cancer treatment outcomes.
  - Source: MOUTH CANCER
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Cancer treatment for mouth cancer is based on tumor location and cancer stage.</data>
