    <node id="FERTILITY PRESERVATION">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">FERTILITY PRESERVATION is the process of keeping options open for having biological children in the future, typically considered and undertaken before starting cancer treatment. It involves specific actions taken prior to cancer therapy to preserve a person’s ability to have children later. This may require a referral to a fertility specialist before treatment begins, so that suitable preservation methods can be discussed and initiated. Available options may include sperm banking for males, as well as counseling and other fertility preservation techniques tailored to the individual’s situation. The goal is to give patients the opportunity to have biological children after cancer treatment. Organizations such as the NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) provide information and guidance on fertility preservation. Overall, fertility preservation is a proactive step that helps maintain future reproductive possibilities for cancer patients.
Relationships: None listed in the data.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
