    <node id="DIAGNOSTIC TESTS">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Diagnostic tests are procedures and tools used to diagnose or evaluate diseases, including cancer. In the context of oncology, they are used to confirm the presence of cancer, determine whether the disease is localized or advanced, and help plan appropriate treatment. These tests also play a critical role in monitoring patients after treatment, as they can detect whether cancer is still growing or spreading. Diagnostic tests include a range of methods such as imaging scans (e.g., CT, MRI, or X-rays), biopsies (removal and examination of tissue samples), and genetic or biomarker tests that analyze DNA, genes, proteins, or other molecular markers. In addition, some diagnostic procedures, such as lumbar puncture, may be used to evaluate diseases affecting the nervous system or to check for the spread of cancer into the cerebrospinal fluid. By providing detailed information about the presence, type, and extent of disease, diagnostic tests are essential for accurate diagnosis, staging, treatment planning, and ongoing surveillance to assess the effectiveness of therapy and detect any recurrence or progression.
Relationships:
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: IMAGING
  - Description: Imaging scans are a type of diagnostic test.
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Biopsies are a type of diagnostic test.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">8</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
Description: Diagnostic tests are procedures and tools used to diagnose or evaluate diseases, including cancer. In the context of oncology, they are used to confirm the presence of cancer, determine whether the disease is localized or advanced, and help plan appropriate treatment. These tests also play a critical role in monitoring patients after treatment, as they can detect whether cancer is still growing or spreading. Diagnostic tests include a range of methods such as imaging scans (e.g., CT, MRI, or X-rays), biopsies (removal and examination of tissue samples), and genetic or biomarker tests that analyze DNA, genes, proteins, or other molecular markers. In addition, some diagnostic procedures, such as lumbar puncture, may be used to evaluate diseases affecting the nervous system or to check for the spread of cancer into the cerebrospinal fluid. By providing detailed information about the presence, type, and extent of disease, diagnostic tests are essential for accurate diagnosis, staging, treatment planning, and ongoing surveillance to assess the effectiveness of therapy and detect any recurrence or progression.
Relationships:
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: IMAGING
  - Description: Imaging scans are a type of diagnostic test.
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Biopsies are a type of diagnostic test.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEDICAL HISTORY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">MEDICAL HISTORY is a comprehensive record of all health issues, treatments, symptoms, and medicines a patient has experienced throughout life. It includes past and current illnesses, infections, injuries, treatments, and lifestyle information, and it serves as a foundational tool for care teams. By reviewing a patient’s medical history, healthcare providers can determine the most appropriate treatment plan, often in combination with a physical examination, imaging studies, and biopsy results. In the context of conditions such as chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), the medical history is specifically used to guide treatment planning alongside other diagnostic information. Essentially, the process of collecting a medical history involves documenting the patient’s health-related events and is essential for making informed decisions about the best course of care.
Relationships:
  - Source: PATIENT
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: The patient provides a medical history to help determine the best treatment
  - Source: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The medical history is a record of a patient’s symptoms, medicines, and related health information, which is shared with the care team during consultations. The care team relies on this medical history to help determine the best treatment plan for the patient. In turn, the patient provides details about symptoms and current medications during the medical history process, enabling the care team to make informed decisions. Together, the medical history and the care team form a collaborative loop: the patient supplies essential information, and the care team uses it to guide treatment.
  - Source: CARE TEAM
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: The care team uses the patient's medical history to help determine which treatment is best. By reviewing the medical history, the care team can assess relevant health information and make informed decisions about the most appropriate course of treatment for the patient.
  - Source: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history and family history are two related but distinct health histories used by the care team. Family history is a specific component of the broader medical history. While the medical history encompasses a patient’s overall health background, the family history focuses specifically on the health conditions and patterns that occur among blood relatives. Both are important for guiding clinical care, but family history contributes to the medical history by providing insight into inherited risks and familial health trends.
  - Source: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history includes family history.
  - Source: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Doctors ask about surgeries during medical history.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history helps determine which treatment is best
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history is collected when evaluating nasopharyngeal cancer
  - Source: CLL
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history is used to plan treatment for CLL.
  - Source: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Target: GENERAL HEALTH TESTS
  - Description: General health tests include taking a medical history
  - Source: GENERAL HEALTH TESTS
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: General health tests encompass a range of evaluations used to assess an individual's overall well-being, and they include the medical history as a fundamental component. Specifically, the medical history is recognized as one of the key general health tests, meaning it is an integral part of the initial health assessment process. Beyond simply being a component, the medical history plays a crucial role in guiding clinical decision-making: it helps determine which additional tests and treatments are relevant and appropriate for the individual. In this way, the medical history both serves as a foundational element of general health testing and shapes the subsequent course of care based on the patient's unique background, prior conditions, and risk factors.
  - Source: TEST RESULTS
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history is one of the sources of test results used to determine the treatment plan
  - Source: TESTING FOR NPC
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history is part of the information gathered in the testing for NPC
  - Source: B SYMPTOMS
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history includes asking about B symptoms.
  - Source: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Target: BLOOD TRANSFUSION
  - Description: Doctors ask about blood transfusions during medical history.</data>
