    <node id="FEVER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">FEVER is recognized in two distinct clinical contexts. First, it serves as a B symptom of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), where a patient experiences fever without any underlying infection or other illness. Second, fever is also a common side effect of targeted therapy, which is used as a cancer treatment. In both cases, the presence of fever is clinically significant, though its cause differs: in CLL it is a disease-related symptom, while in targeted therapy it is an adverse reaction to medication.
Relationships: (None listed)</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
