    <node id="EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY">
      <data key="d3">9</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">(No description provided in the data)
Relationships:
  - Source: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: RADIATION-INDUCED MENOPAUSE
  - Description: External beam radiation therapy can cause radiation-induced menopause when the ovaries are in the radiation field.
  - Source: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Target: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
  - Description: Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure performed prior to external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) to reposition one or both ovaries out of the radiation beam's range. The goal is to reduce radiation damage to the ovaries during EBRT, thereby preserving ovarian function. By moving the ovaries away from the targeted radiation field, the procedure minimizes the risk of radiation-induced ovarian failure. External beam radiation therapy, in turn, is the treatment modality that delivers targeted radiation to a specific area; ovarian transposition serves as a protective measure for patients undergoing this therapy. Together, the two entities represent a combined strategy to safeguard reproductive health while still benefiting from cancer treatment.
  - Source: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Description: Ovarian transposition moves one or both ovaries out of the range of the external beam radiation therapy beam.
  - Source: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: OVARIES
  - Description: If ovaries are included in the radiation field, EBRT can cause premature menopause.</data>
