    <node id="PATHOLOGIST">
      <data key="d3">3</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">A pathologist is a physician and medical professional who specializes in examining cells and tissues removed during a biopsy to detect and diagnose disease, particularly cancer. Using a microscope, the pathologist analyzes biopsy samples to identify abnormal cells, determine the presence of cancer, and provide crucial diagnostic information. This includes establishing a cancer diagnosis, determining the stage of a cancerous tumor, and performing biomarker testing that guides treatment decisions. The pathologist carefully notes the size, shape, and type of cells in the sample, and in certain cases—such as prostate biopsies—may measure the percentage of cancer present in each tissue core. For skin biopsies, the pathologist specifically evaluates the tissue to diagnose conditions like squamous cell skin cancer and determine the exact type and subtype of cancer. After completing the analysis, the pathologist writes a detailed pathology report summarizing the findings, which serves as an essential reference for other doctors managing the patient's care. In all cases, the pathologist acts as a specialist who transforms biopsy tissue into actionable medical information, ensuring accurate diagnosis and informed treatment planning.
Relationships: None listed</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
