    <node id="ESOPHAGEAL CANCER">
      <data key="d3">9</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Esophageal cancer is a malignancy that arises in the esophagus, the muscular tube that connects the throat to the stomach and carries food and liquid during swallowing. It is commonly categorized as a form of throat cancer, and recognized treatment guidance is provided by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN), which offers patient-focused clinical guidelines for managing the disease. Esophageal cancer is characterized by a slow development within the esophagus, frequently preceded by precancerous changes in the esophageal lining. The disease is staged according to its extent, typically classified as early-stage, locoregional (confined to the esophagus and nearby tissues or lymph nodes), or metastatic (spread to distant organs). This staging system is essential for determining appropriate treatment approaches and prognosis. Overall, esophageal cancer represents a serious condition requiring careful monitoring and multidisciplinary care.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: ESOPHAGEAL CANCER
  - Target: THROAT CANCER
  - Description: The text lists esophageal cancer among other types of cancer that affect the throat
  - Source: LARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: ESOPHAGEAL CANCER
  - Description: The text lists laryngeal cancer and esophageal cancer as other types of cancer that affect the throat</data>
