    <node id="LEUKEMIA">
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">LEUKEMIA is a cancer of blood-forming tissues, characterized by the abnormal proliferation of blood cells. This disease can spread beyond the bloodstream to other parts of the body, including the lymph nodes, liver, spleen, and skin, and, in rare cases, it may affect the lining of the brain and spinal cord. Additionally, leukemia can travel to the cerebrospinal fluid surrounding the spine or brain, where it may cause symptoms such as headaches, neck pain, and sensitivity to light. The development of leukemia can be influenced by inherited leukemia predisposition syndromes, which contribute to an individual's genetic risk for the disease.
Relationships: (None listed in the provided data)</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: LEUKEMIA
Description: LEUKEMIA is a cancer of blood-forming tissues, characterized by the abnormal proliferation of blood cells. This disease can spread beyond the bloodstream to other parts of the body, including the lymph nodes, liver, spleen, and skin, and, in rare cases, it may affect the lining of the brain and spinal cord. Additionally, leukemia can travel to the cerebrospinal fluid surrounding the spine or brain, where it may cause symptoms such as headaches, neck pain, and sensitivity to light. The development of leukemia can be influenced by inherited leukemia predisposition syndromes, which contribute to an individual's genetic risk for the disease.
Relationships: (None listed in the provided data)</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUITTING SMOKING">
      <data key="d3">13</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Quitting smoking is the process of stopping the use of tobacco cigarettes and other smoked products. It is a significant health intervention that may reduce cancer risk and improve outcomes for cancer treatment, particularly for bladder cancer. The act of quitting smoking is supported by a range of resources, including counseling, medications, and online tools, all of which can help individuals succeed in their efforts. By discontinuing smoking, individuals can experience better cancer treatment results and lower their overall risk of developing cancer. This comprehensive approach highlights both the health benefits and the practical support available for those seeking to quit.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Target: SMOKING
  - Description: Quitting smoking is the act of stopping smoking.
  - Source: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Target: NICOTINE WITHDRAWAL
  - Description: Nicotine withdrawal is challenging for people who smoke and try to quit.
  - Source: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Quitting smoking can make cancer treatment work better.
  - Source: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Target: CANCER
  - Description: Quitting smoking can lead to better cancer treatment outcomes.
