    <node id="METASTASIS">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">METASTASIS is the biological process by which cancer cells break away from the primary tumor and travel through the bloodstream or lymphatic system to form new tumors in other parts of the body. It is the mechanism by which cancer spreads from its original site to distant areas, and the presence of metastasis is often what defines advanced or metastatic cancer. In clinical staging, metastasis is assessed as the "M" component in the TNM (or AJCC TNM) staging system, and in some cases corresponds to stage 4 cancer. Metastasis can involve nearby lymph nodes or distant organs, most commonly the liver, lungs, bones, abdomen, chest, or other distant lymph nodes. It is often detected through imaging tests such as PET scans and bone scans. The specific behaviors of metastasis vary by cancer type. In prostate cancer, metastasis refers to the spread of cancer cells outside the prostate to other parts of the body, including lymph nodes, bones, liver, and lungs; this defines advanced-stage prostate cancer. In anal cancer, metastasis is the spread of cancer beyond the pelvis—most often to the liver or lungs—and may also occur through lymphatics or blood to distant lymph nodes, chest, or abdomen; this is treated with platinum-based chemotherapy. In colon cancer, metastasis is the spread of cancer to areas or organs outside the colon, most often the liver and/or lungs, and is assessed as the M component in the AJCC TNM staging system. Basal cell skin cancer can also metastasize to lymph nodes or distant parts of the body, while very high-risk cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) carries an increased risk of metastasis to distant sites. Metastasis may also occur or be discovered after initial cancer treatment. In such cases, the presence and location of metastasis influence whether radiation therapy can be used again and whether palliative radiation is an appropriate option. Overall, metastasis represents the systemic spread of cancer and is a key factor in prognosis, staging, and treatment planning.
Relationships:
  - Source: METASTASIS
