  - Description: Prostate cancer can spread to other areas of the body through metastasis, and this spread is called advanced-stage disease
  - Source: PROSTATE CANCER
  - Target: METASTASIS
  - Description: Prostate cancer can advance by metastasis, a process defined as the spread of cancer cells outside the prostate to other organs and tissues. This form of progression is characteristic of advanced-stage prostate cancer. Metastasis occurs when cancer cells travel through the bloodstream or the lymphatic system, enabling the cancer to disseminate beyond the prostate to distant sites in the body. In this way, prostate cancer metastasis involves the movement of malignant cells from the prostate via circulatory or lymphatic routes, ultimately establishing secondary growths in other organs and tissues.
