    <node id="MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY">
      <data key="d3">4</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Microsatellite instability (MSI) is a biomarker that reflects defective DNA mismatch repair (dMMR). In normal cells, mismatch repair corrects errors that occur during DNA replication, ensuring that short repeated DNA sequences, known as microsatellites, remain stable. When this repair system fails, these errors are not corrected, causing the repeated sequences to change length. This instability is the basis of MSI.

MSI can be measured in a clinical setting; for example, it is tested in recurrent ovarian cancer as a biomarker. When MSI is elevated, the condition is referred to as MSI-H (high microsatellite instability). MSI is closely related to dMMR, as the presence of defective mismatch repair is the underlying cause of microsatellite instability. In summary, microsatellite instability occurs when DNA mismatch repair errors are not fixed, leading to alterations in the length of short, repeated DNA sequences, and this phenomenon is directly linked to the dMMR and MSI-H designations.
Relationships:
  - Source: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Target: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY
  - Description: MSI-H/dMMR mutation is a type of DNA change that leads to microsatellite instability-high or deficient mismatch repair. Microsatellite instability is a characteristic feature of MSI-H/dMMR mutation. In other words, the MSI-H/dMMR mutation drives the development of microsatellite instability, making the two closely related in the context of genomic alterations.
  - Source: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY
  - Target: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR
  - Description: Microsatellite instability (MSI) is a condition characterized by the accumulation of genetic alterations in microsatellite regions—short, repetitive DNA sequences prone to errors during replication. Deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) is a key underlying cause of this instability. Specifically, MSI is often due to defects in the mismatch repair system, which normally corrects errors that occur during DNA replication. When the genes responsible for mismatch repair are deficient, replication errors in microsatellites are not corrected, leading to the high mutation rate and variability observed in these regions. Thus, microsatellite instability is frequently attributed to deficient mismatch repair genes, highlighting the direct relationship between the two entities: dMMR drives MSI, and MSI serves as a molecular indicator of defective mismatch repair function. This connection is important in contexts such as cancer diagnosis and treatment, where MSI/dMMR status can influence prognosis and therapeutic decisions. Overall, microsatellite instability often arises from deficient mismatch repair, and the two terms are closely linked in clinical and biological understanding.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
