    <node id="SURGICAL STAGING">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">SURGICAL STAGING, also known as pathologic staging, is a procedure performed during surgery to determine the extent of cancer, most notably ovarian cancer. It is considered the most accurate method for staging ovarian cancer. During the procedure, a surgeon removes and tests lymph nodes and takes tissue samples from organs and areas where the cancer may have spread. Specifically, para-aortic lymph nodes are typically removed, while pelvic lymph nodes may or may not be removed depending on the case. This process allows a pathologist to assess tumor growth and lymph node involvement, ultimately assigning a cancer stage of I, II, or III. While the procedure is often minimally invasive, it is fundamentally a surgical intervention that provides critical information for treatment planning and prognosis.
Relationships: (No relationships listed in the data)</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">5</data>
