    <node id="OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Ovarian transposition, also medically termed oophoropexy, is a surgical procedure that moves one or both ovaries out of the range of a radiation beam in order to protect them during radiation therapy. This procedure is specifically used to shield the ovaries from radiation exposure, thereby helping to preserve ovarian function. In premenopausal women with squamous cell carcinoma, ovarian transposition may reduce the risk of radiation-induced menopause. Overall, the surgery aims to safeguard fertility and hormonal function by relocating the ovaries away from the targeted radiation field.
Relationships:
  - Source: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Target: RADIATION-INDUCED MENOPAUSE
  - Description: Ovarian transposition may reduce or prevent radiation-induced menopause.
  - Source: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Target: OVARIES
  - Description: Ovarian transposition moves one or both ovaries out of the radiation beam range.
  - Source: OVARIAN TISSUE BANKING
  - Target: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Description: Both ovarian tissue banking and ovarian transposition are described as fertility preservation options for people who may want children after treatment.
  - Source: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Target: OOPHOROPEXY
  - Description: Ovarian transposition is the surgery medically named oophoropexy; they are the same procedure.
  - Source: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Target: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
  - Description: Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure performed prior to external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) to reposition one or both ovaries out of the radiation beam's range. The goal is to reduce radiation damage to the ovaries during EBRT, thereby preserving ovarian function. By moving the ovaries away from the targeted radiation field, the procedure minimizes the risk of radiation-induced ovarian failure. External beam radiation therapy, in turn, is the treatment modality that delivers targeted radiation to a specific area; ovarian transposition serves as a protective measure for patients undergoing this therapy. Together, the two entities represent a combined strategy to safeguard reproductive health while still benefiting from cancer treatment.
  - Source: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Description: Ovarian transposition moves one or both ovaries out of the range of the external beam radiation therapy beam.
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Description: Ovarian transposition may be an option for cervical cancer patients receiving EBRT
  - Source: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Target: FERTILITY COUNSELING
  - Description: Fertility counseling discusses fertility preservation options, including ovarian transposition.
  - Source: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Target: SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA
  - Description: Ovarian transposition may be an option for premenopausal women with squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
