    <node id="FDA">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a federal agency responsible for protecting public health by regulating and approving new drugs, medical devices, and treatment approaches. The FDA may grant approval for these products after they are found to be safe and effective in clinical trials, specifically following successful Phase 3 clinical trial results. In addition to its role in initial approvals, the FDA also monitors FDA-approved treatments during Phase 4 trials to ensure their ongoing safety and effectiveness in real-world use. This comprehensive oversight makes the FDA a central authority in the U.S. healthcare system.
Relationships:
  - Source: FDA
  - Target: PHASE 1 TRIALS
  - Description: Phase 1 trials are the earliest phase of clinical trials, and the drug or approach tested may later be submitted for FDA approval if it is safe and effective
  - Source: FDA
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Description: If a new drug, device, or treatment approach is found to be safe and effective in a clinical trial, it may be approved by the FDA
If a new drug, device, or treatment approach is found safe and effective in a clinical trial, it may be approved by the FDA
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Target: FDA
  - Description: Clinical trials give people access to investigational treatments that may, in time, be approved by the FDA.
FDA may approve a treatment after Phase 3 clinical trial results are good, and Phase 4 trials study safety of FDA-approved treatments
  - Source: FDA
  - Target: UNITED STATES
  - Description: The FDA, or the U.S. Food and Drug Administration, is a federal agency of the United States. It serves as a U.S. regulatory agency responsible for approving cancer treatments, among its broader public health regulatory duties.
  - Source: FDA
  - Target: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Description: NCI provides information on clinical trials that may eventually lead to FDA-approved treatments
  - Source: FDA
  - Target: PHASE 2 TRIALS
  - Description: Phase 2 trials test how well a drug works against a specific cancer, and positive results may contribute to later FDA approval
  - Source: FDA
  - Target: PHASE 3 TRIALS
  - Description: The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is the regulatory authority responsible for approving new treatments and therapies. Phase 3 trials are a critical stage in the clinical research process, designed to test a drug or treatment approach against a standard existing treatment to determine its effectiveness, safety, and overall benefit. If the results of a Phase 3 trial are favorable—meaning the new treatment demonstrates sufficient efficacy and an acceptable safety profile—the treatment approach may be submitted for approval by the FDA. Thus, successful Phase 3 trial outcomes are a key prerequisite for FDA approval, allowing the treatment to become available to patients.
  - Source: FDA
  - Target: PHASE 4 TRIALS
  - Description: The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and Phase 4 trials are closely related in the context of post-approval drug and treatment monitoring. Phase 4 trials are conducted after a treatment has already received FDA approval. These trials focus on studying the long-term safety and benefit of the FDA-approved treatment. By gathering additional data over an extended period, Phase 4 trials help confirm that the treatment continues to be safe and effective in real-world patient populations, beyond the controlled conditions of earlier clinical trial phases. This ongoing evaluation supports the FDA's broader mission of ensuring that approved medical products maintain a favorable risk-benefit profile once they are available to the public. In summary, Phase 4 trials serve as a crucial post-marketing surveillance mechanism, with the FDA overseeing and relying on their findings to monitor the long-term safety and benefit of approved treatments.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
