    <node id="PHASE 3 TRIALS">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Phase 3 trials are a phase of clinical trials in which a drug or treatment approach is tested against a standard treatment. Positive results from these trials may lead to approval by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA).
Relationships:
  - Source: FDA
  - Target: PHASE 3 TRIALS
  - Description: The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is the regulatory authority responsible for approving new treatments and therapies. Phase 3 trials are a critical stage in the clinical research process, designed to test a drug or treatment approach against a standard existing treatment to determine its effectiveness, safety, and overall benefit. If the results of a Phase 3 trial are favorable—meaning the new treatment demonstrates sufficient efficacy and an acceptable safety profile—the treatment approach may be submitted for approval by the FDA. Thus, successful Phase 3 trial outcomes are a key prerequisite for FDA approval, allowing the treatment to become available to patients.</data>
