    <node id="FATIGUE">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">FATIGUE is a recognized side effect of cancer treatments. It commonly occurs during the course of external beam radiation therapy, typically appearing around the fifth to sixth week of treatment. Fatigue may also begin after radiation treatment has ended and can persist for several weeks. In addition to radiation therapy, fatigue is associated with other treatment modalities, including targeted therapy, immunotherapy, and high-dose chemotherapy. This side effect is shared across multiple cancer treatment approaches and can affect patients at various stages of their treatment journey.
Relationships: (None listed in the data tables)</data>
