    <node id="IN SUMMARY, SURGERY AND CHEMOTHERAPY ARE BOTH ESSENTIAL AND COMPLEMENTARY TOOLS IN TREATING OVARIAN CANCER. THEIR OPTIMAL SEQUENCE IS DETERMINED BY DISEASE STAGE AND RESECTABILITY: IF SURGERY CANNOT BE PERFORMED FIRST DUE TO EXTENSIVE SPREAD, CHEMOTHERAPY IS ADMINISTERED BEFORE SURGERY; OTHERWISE, SURGERY IS PERFORMED FIRST AND FOLLOWED BY CHEMOTHERAPY. THE COMBINED EXPERTISE OF A GYNECOLOGIC ONCOLOGIST COVERS BOTH MODALITIES, ENSURING THAT PATIENTS RECEIVE THE MOST APPROPRIATE AND INDIVIDUALIZED TREATMENT PLAN.">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Chemotherapy is a treatment option for cervical cancer, and it is also used as part of a broader treatment approach for this condition. In some cases, chemotherapy may be the primary treatment, while in others it is combined with other therapies such as surgery or radiation. Thus, chemotherapy plays a role both as a standalone option and as a component of a multidisciplinary treatment plan for cervical cancer.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The care team talks with the patient about the types of chemotherapy, doses, timing, and side effects
  - Source: CARE TEAM
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: The care team helps manage side effects such as nausea and vomiting during chemotherapy.
  - Source: TREATMENT
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a form of medical treatment used primarily in oncology. As a cancer treatment, chemotherapy involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It is applicable across multiple cancer types and is often integrated into broader therapeutic protocols. Specifically, chemotherapy is a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it serves as the standard-of-care approach for managing this blood cancer. In addition to its role in AML, chemotherapy is also a component of cervical cancer treatment, frequently administered as part of chemoradiation—a combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy that enhances the effectiveness of treatment. As such, chemotherapy occupies a central position within the overall category of cancer treatment, functioning both as a standalone modality and as an adjunct to other therapies. The entity TREATMENT encompasses a wide range of medical interventions, and chemotherapy is one of its key subtypes, distinguished by its mechanism of action and its established role in hematologic and solid tumor malignancies. Overall, chemotherapy exemplifies a targeted and evidence-based treatment option, with particular significance in AML, where it is the established standard of care, and in cervical cancer, where it is integrated into combined-modality regimens.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Chemotherapy regimens are recommended for use with radiation therapy to treat stage I, II, and III anal cancer
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CANCER
  - Description: Chemotherapy uses anti-cancer medicines that travel in the bloodstream to reach cells throughout the body, including cancer cells
  - Source: PATIENT
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy can affect the patient's fertility
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Description: The clinical trial could involve treatment with chemotherapy.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: METASTASIS
  - Description: Metastatic anal cancer is treated with platinum-based chemotherapy
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CHEMORADIATION
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a cancer treatment that uses drugs to kill or slow the growth of cancer cells. Chemoradiation is a treatment approach that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. In this combined regimen, chemotherapy serves as one of the core components, specifically functioning as the chemotherapy portion of the treatment. Chemoradiation may be followed by additional chemotherapy, which is given to kill any lingering cancer cells that remain after the initial combined treatment.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: OVARIAN CANCER
  - Description: Chemotherapy is one of the main treatments for ovarian cancer. In cases where ovarian cancer has spread too extensively for initial surgery, chemotherapy is needed first.
  - Source: CHEMORADIATION
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemoradiation is a treatment modality that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. Specifically, chemoradiation includes chemotherapy administered concurrently with radiation therapy, meaning the drug component is given at the same time as the radiation treatment. In the context of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), chemotherapy serves as the drug component delivered concurrently with EBRT as part of the chemoradiation regimen. The available descriptions indicate that chemoradiation, chemotherapy, or both may be listed as possible next treatment options for a patient, depending on the clinical scenario. Chemotherapy, as the drug component, works in conjunction with radiation to enhance the overall treatment effect. Thus, chemoradiation represents the integration of systemic drug therapy and localized radiation, while chemotherapy itself can also be considered independently as a treatment option.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment and one component of the broader category of cancer treatment. It is used to combat cancer but can have side effects, notably affecting fertility. Thus, while chemotherapy is a key part of cancer treatment, it is not the entirety of it, and its impact on fertility is an important consideration for patients.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a form of cancer treatment.
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a treatment for head and neck cancer that can affect fertility
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: SYSTEMIC THERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a form of systemic therapy. Systemic therapy is a treatment approach that uses substances that travel through the bloodstream, reaching and affecting cells throughout the entire body rather than targeting a single, localized area. Chemotherapy specifically involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy rapidly dividing cells, most commonly cancer cells, by interfering with their ability to grow and multiply. Because chemotherapy is a systemic therapy, it can treat cancer that has spread to multiple sites in the body, unlike localized treatments such as surgery or radiation therapy, which focus on a specific region. In summary, chemotherapy and systemic therapy are closely related: chemotherapy is one specific type of systemic therapy, and systemic therapy is the broader category encompassing treatments that work throughout the body.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a main treatment for primary CNS lymphoma
  - Source: SYSTEMIC THERAPY
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a specific type of systemic therapy. As a systemic therapy, chemotherapy is administered intravenously and travels through the bloodstream, allowing it to reach cells throughout the body rather than targeting only one localized area. This broad distribution is a defining characteristic of systemic treatment. Chemotherapy is also one of the most common types of systemic therapy used for bladder cancer. Thus, while systemic therapy is the broader category encompassing various treatment approaches that affect the whole body, chemotherapy is a prominent and widely utilized form of that category, particularly in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Chemotherapy is a treatment option for cervical cancer, and it is also used as part of a broader treatment approach for this condition. In some cases, chemotherapy may be the primary treatment, while in others it is combined with other therapies such as surgery or radiation. Thus, chemotherapy plays a role both as a standalone option and as a component of a multidisciplinary treatment plan for cervical cancer.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The care team talks with the patient about the types of chemotherapy, doses, timing, and side effects
  - Source: CARE TEAM
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: The care team helps manage side effects such as nausea and vomiting during chemotherapy.
  - Source: TREATMENT
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a form of medical treatment used primarily in oncology. As a cancer treatment, chemotherapy involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It is applicable across multiple cancer types and is often integrated into broader therapeutic protocols. Specifically, chemotherapy is a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it serves as the standard-of-care approach for managing this blood cancer. In addition to its role in AML, chemotherapy is also a component of cervical cancer treatment, frequently administered as part of chemoradiation—a combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy that enhances the effectiveness of treatment. As such, chemotherapy occupies a central position within the overall category of cancer treatment, functioning both as a standalone modality and as an adjunct to other therapies. The entity TREATMENT encompasses a wide range of medical interventions, and chemotherapy is one of its key subtypes, distinguished by its mechanism of action and its established role in hematologic and solid tumor malignancies. Overall, chemotherapy exemplifies a targeted and evidence-based treatment option, with particular significance in AML, where it is the established standard of care, and in cervical cancer, where it is integrated into combined-modality regimens.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Chemotherapy regimens are recommended for use with radiation therapy to treat stage I, II, and III anal cancer
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
