    <node id="CANCER CARE TEAM">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The cancer care team is the patient's dedicated medical team responsible for guiding and managing cancer treatment. This team decides on the appropriate treatment plan, and as part of its comprehensive care, it performs pregnancy tests before starting any cancer treatment to ensure patient safety. The team also assesses fertility risks associated with the proposed treatment, offering referrals to address fertility preservation or related concerns. In addition, the cancer care team communicates important diagnoses to the patient, such as Richter transformation, ensuring that patients are fully informed about their condition. Overall, this team provides holistic cancer care, encompassing diagnosis, treatment planning, pre-treatment screening, fertility considerations, and ongoing communication throughout the patient's cancer journey.
Relationships:
  - Source: CANCER CARE TEAM
  - Target: FERTILITY SPECIALIST
  - Description: The cancer care team may give the patient a referral to a fertility specialist to discuss fertility preservation.
  - Source: CANCER CARE TEAM
  - Target: FERTILITY COUNSELOR
  - Description: The cancer care team advises the patient to talk with a fertility counselor before starting treatment to understand fertility preservation options.
  - Source: PREGNANCY TEST
  - Target: CANCER CARE TEAM
  - Description: The cancer care team gives a pregnancy test before treatment.
  - Source: CANCER CARE TEAM
  - Target: WATCH AND WAIT
  - Description: The cancer care team decides or advises that the patient may move to the watch-and-wait stage based on test results.
  - Source: CANCER CARE TEAM
  - Target: RICHTER TRANSFORMATION
  - Description: The cancer care team tells the patient if they have Richter transformation and directs them to read Chapter 5.
  - Source: CLL SPECIALIST
  - Target: CANCER CARE TEAM
  - Description: The CLL specialist is involved in the patient's CLL care and shares patient-management responsibilities with the broader cancer care team.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">6</data>
