    <node id="5 INCHES (4 CENTIMETERS) LONG, AND IT IS THE PRIMARY AREA WHERE ANAL CANCER DEVELOPS [DATA: ENTITIES (132); RELATIONSHIPS (241)]. THE ANUS IS THE OPENING AT THE END OF THE DIGESTIVE TRACT THROUGH WHICH STOOL EXITS THE BODY [DATA: ENTITIES (131); RELATIONSHIPS (240)]. ANAL CANCER CAN BE CLASSIFIED AS STAGE I, II, OR III, AND THE STANDARD TREATMENT APPROACH INVOLVES CHEMORADIATION [DATA: ENTITIES (99)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
Anal canal cancer is a specific form of anal cancer that is non-metastatic and for which chemoradiation is the recommended primary treatment [Data: Entities (150); Relationships (283, 255)]. Anal squamous cell carcinoma is an invasive form of anal cancer confirmed by biopsy, more advanced than SISCCA (superficially invasive squamous cell carcinoma), and requires additional staging [Data: Entities (121); Relationships (211, 212)]. SISCCA is described as the earliest form of anal cancer, with lesions typically smaller than 7 mm wide and not deep at all [Data: Entities (99); Relationships (207)].
</data>
    </node>
