    <node id="INVASIVE BREAST CANCER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">INVASIVE BREAST CANCER is a form of breast cancer that is covered in an NCCN patient guideline. It is defined as breast cancer that has spread from the milk ducts or milk glands (lobules) into the surrounding breast tissue or nearby lymph nodes. From there, it can potentially spread through the lymphatic system or bloodstream to other parts of the body. This condition is characterized by its ability to invade adjacent healthy tissue, distinguishing it from non-invasive (in situ) breast cancer.
Relationships:
  - Source: INVASIVE BREAST CANCER
  - Target: METASTASIS
  - Description: Once outside the ducts or lobules, invasive breast cancer can spread through lymph or blood to other parts of the body through metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
