  - Source: BREAST CANCER
  - Target: DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU
  - Description: Ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) is a form of breast cancer, specifically classified as a noninvasive, stage 0 condition. As a breast cancer subtype, DCIS is characterized by the presence of abnormal cells that are confined within the milk ducts of the breast and have not spread into surrounding breast tissue. This noninvasive nature distinguishes it from invasive breast cancers, and it is considered the earliest stage of breast cancer development.
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU
  - Description: Physical exam is listed as part of testing for DCIS.
  - Source: TREATMENT PLANNING
  - Target: DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU
  - Description: Treatment planning starts with testing for DCIS and aims to treat the condition to prevent invasive breast cancer.
  - Source: DUCTAL CARCINOMA
  - Target: DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU
  - Description: Ductal carcinoma in situ is a ductal carcinoma that is in situ
  - Source: INVASIVE BREAST CANCER
  - Target: DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU
  - Description: DCIS is treated to prevent the development of invasive breast cancer, a more advanced form of cancer.</data>
