    <node id="PROCTOSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Proctoscopy is a medical procedure that uses a proctoscope to visually examine the anus and rectum. It is often performed under anesthesia and may be needed to look for signs of cancer in the bowel. In addition to its diagnostic role, proctoscopy serves as a surveillance examination following transanal local excision. The recommended surveillance schedule includes examinations every 3 to 6 months for the first 2 years, followed by every 6 months for the next 3 years, covering a total period of 5 years after the procedure. This comprehensive approach helps monitor for recurrence or signs of cancer in the bowel.
Relationships:
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Both are procedures used under anesthesia to examine organs for signs of cancer.
  - Source: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Target: COLONOSCOPY
  - Description: Both are endoscopic surveillance procedures recommended after transanal local excision; proctoscopy is more frequent and colonoscopy is done at longer intervals.
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Proctoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the anus and rectum, and it plays a role in evaluating certain cancers. Specifically, proctoscopy is used to look for signs of cancer in the bowel. In the context of cervical cancer, proctoscopy may be needed to look for signs of cervical cancer in the bowel. Additionally, proctoscopy may be used to check the anus and rectum for cervical cancer spread, helping to determine whether the cancer has metastasized to these areas. Thus, for cervical cancer patients, proctoscopy serves as a diagnostic tool to assess possible bowel involvement and local spread of the disease.
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Proctoscopy is a surveillance examination performed after transanal local excision.
  - Source: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Target: TESTING
  - Description: Proctoscopy is a testing procedure used to look for signs of cancer in the bowel.
  - Source: TRANSANAL LOCAL EXCISION
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Proctoscopy is recommended for 5 years after transanal local excision to monitor for recurrence.
  - Source: EXAMINATIONS UNDER ANESTHESIA
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Proctoscopy is one of the examinations that can be performed under anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">7</data>
