    <node id="ANOSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">ANOSCOPY is a medical procedure used to inspect the anal canal and lower rectum using an anoscope. It is typically performed as a surveillance exam during follow-up care for individuals treated for anal cancer, including during remission. The procedure is recommended every 6 to 12 months for a period of 3 years after treatment, when feasible. During follow-up, anoscopy is often combined with a digital rectal examination to ensure thorough assessment. In cases where treatment was limited to lymph nodes only, anoscopy is still recommended on the same schedule, every 6 to 12 months for 3 years; however, it may be omitted if the procedure causes significant pain, particularly after radiation therapy.
Relationships: (None listed in the provided data)</data>
