    <node id="PALLIATIVE RADIATION THERAPY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Palliative radiation therapy is a form of treatment used to limit tumor size and alleviate symptoms when cancer is causing distress. Its primary goal is not to cure the cancer, but to improve the patient's quality of life by reducing pain, pressure, or other symptoms resulting from the tumor. This approach carefully avoids re-treating previously irradiated areas, thereby minimizing additional harm and respecting prior radiation exposure.
Relationships:
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: PALLIATIVE RADIATION THERAPY
  - Description: RADIATION THERAPY is a broad medical treatment modality that uses ionizing radiation to treat various conditions, most notably cancer. PALLIATIVE RADIATION THERAPY is a specific subtype of radiation therapy. It is defined as a type of radiation therapy used to control symptoms, rather than to cure the underlying disease. Palliative radiation therapy aims to relieve pain, reduce pressure, or address other distressing symptoms caused by tumors or malignancies, thereby improving the patient's quality of life. As a form of radiation therapy, it shares the same fundamental principles and techniques, but its primary goal is symptom management and supportive care in advanced or incurable disease settings. Together, these descriptions establish that palliative radiation therapy is a specialized application of radiation therapy focused on alleviating symptoms.
  - Source: METASTASIS
  - Target: PALLIATIVE RADIATION THERAPY
  - Description: If metastatic cancer causes symptoms, palliative radiation therapy may be used to limit tumor size and help with symptoms.

---</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
