    <node id="BURKITT LYMPHOMA">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Burkitt lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma. It is widely recognized as essentially the same disease as Burkitt leukemia, which is also referred to as mature B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) or Burkitt leukemia/mature B-cell ALL. These terms describe the same underlying condition, with the distinction primarily based on the extent of bone marrow and blood involvement at presentation. Burkitt lymphoma is thus considered equivalent to Burkitt leukemia and mature B-cell ALL in clinical and pathological terms.
Relationships:
  - Source: MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)
  - Target: BURKITT LYMPHOMA
  - Description: Burkitt leukemia is essentially the same as Burkitt lymphoma, a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma.
  - Source: BURKITT LYMPHOMA
  - Target: NON-HODGKIN LYMPHOMA
  - Description: Burkitt lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma. This means that Burkitt lymphoma belongs to the broader classification of non-Hodgkin lymphoma, which encompasses a diverse group of blood cancers that arise from lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell. As a non-Hodgkin lymphoma, Burkitt lymphoma shares certain characteristics with other lymphomas in this category, such as its origin in the lymphatic system, but it also has distinct clinical and pathological features. The classification of Burkitt lymphoma as a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma is fundamental to understanding its diagnosis, treatment approach, and prognosis.
  - Source: BURKITT LEUKEMIA
  - Target: BURKITT LYMPHOMA
  - Description: Burkitt leukemia and Burkitt lymphoma are closely related conditions. Burkitt leukemia is essentially the same disease as Burkitt lymphoma, sharing the same underlying pathology. However, despite this fundamental similarity, the two are treated differently in clinical practice. The distinction reflects differences in how the disease presents and the treatment approach chosen, but the core disease process is considered the same entity.
  - Source: NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK
  - Target: BURKITT LYMPHOMA
  - Description: The NCCN Guidelines mention Burkitt lymphoma when describing mature B-cell ALL.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">4</data>
