    <node id="TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND">
      <data key="d3">8</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Transvaginal ultrasound is a type of imaging test in which a probe is inserted into the vagina. This approach allows the doctor to see the ovaries more clearly than with some other methods. It is also used as an alternative when a pelvic MRI cannot be performed. In the context of cervical cancer, transvaginal ultrasound may be used as part of initial imaging, and it serves as an initial imaging test to determine the extent of the disease before treatment begins.
Relationships:
  - Source: OVARIAN CANCER
  - Target: TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Transvaginal ultrasound helps the doctor see the ovaries more clearly when looking for ovarian cancer.
  - Source: IMAGING
  - Target: TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Transvaginal ultrasound is an initial imaging modality within the broader field of imaging. Specifically, it is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging, serving as a key diagnostic tool for evaluating the extent of disease.
  - Source: MRI
  - Target: TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Transvaginal ultrasound may be used when MRI of the pelvis cannot be done.
  - Source: ULTRASOUND
  - Target: TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Transvaginal ultrasound is one of the two types of ultrasound used for ovarian cancer.
  - Source: TRANSABDOMINAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND
  - Description: The text describes both as the two types of ultrasound that may be used to look for ovarian cancer.</data>
