    <node id="ADENOCARCINOMA">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Adenocarcinoma is a form of cancer that arises from glandular tissues, specifically developing in cells that produce mucus or other secretions. It is recognized as a distinct subtype in several different organs, each with its own clinical context.

In the bladder, adenocarcinoma is a non-urothelial type of bladder cancer, meaning it does not originate from the transitional epithelial cells typically associated with bladder cancer. Instead, it forms in glandular tissue within the bladder.

In the cervix, adenocarcinoma is a type of cervical cancer that develops in the glandular cells lining the endocervical canal. This distinguishes it from squamous cell carcinoma, the more common form of cervical cancer.

In the esophagus, adenocarcinoma begins in the mucus-secreting cells that line the inside of the esophagus. It is often linked to chronic acid reflux and Barrett's esophagus, and it represents one of the major histological types of esophageal cancer.

In the colon, adenocarcinoma is the most common type of colon cancer. It forms in an adenoma, which is a benign polyp, and can become malignant as it progresses. This pathway is central to the development of colorectal cancer.

Overall, adenocarcinoma is a broad classification of cancer that shares a glandular origin but manifests in various organs, including the bladder, cervix, esophagus, and colon. Each site exhibits unique characteristics and risk factors, but all involve malignant transformation of glandular cells.
Relationships:
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: ADENOCARCINOMA
  - Description: Adenocarcinoma is a type of cervical cancer that forms in glandular cells.
  - Source: ADENOCARCINOMA
  - Target: BLADDER CANCER
  - Description: Adenocarcinoma is a type of bladder cancer
  - Source: ESOPHAGEAL CANCER
  - Target: ADENOCARCINOMA
  - Description: Adenocarcinoma is a subtype of esophageal cancer
  - Source: ADENOMA
  - Target: ADENOCARCINOMA
  - Description: Cancer that forms in an adenoma is called adenocarcinoma

---</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">4</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: ADENOCARCINOMA
Description: Adenocarcinoma is a form of cancer that arises from glandular tissues, specifically developing in cells that produce mucus or other secretions. It is recognized as a distinct subtype in several different organs, each with its own clinical context.

In the bladder, adenocarcinoma is a non-urothelial type of bladder cancer, meaning it does not originate from the transitional epithelial cells typically associated with bladder cancer. Instead, it forms in glandular tissue within the bladder.

In the cervix, adenocarcinoma is a type of cervical cancer that develops in the glandular cells lining the endocervical canal. This distinguishes it from squamous cell carcinoma, the more common form of cervical cancer.

In the esophagus, adenocarcinoma begins in the mucus-secreting cells that line the inside of the esophagus. It is often linked to chronic acid reflux and Barrett's esophagus, and it represents one of the major histological types of esophageal cancer.

In the colon, adenocarcinoma is the most common type of colon cancer. It forms in an adenoma, which is a benign polyp, and can become malignant as it progresses. This pathway is central to the development of colorectal cancer.

Overall, adenocarcinoma is a broad classification of cancer that shares a glandular origin but manifests in various organs, including the bladder, cervix, esophagus, and colon. Each site exhibits unique characteristics and risk factors, but all involve malignant transformation of glandular cells.
Relationships:
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: ADENOCARCINOMA
  - Description: Adenocarcinoma is a type of cervical cancer that forms in glandular cells.
  - Source: ADENOCARCINOMA
  - Target: BLADDER CANCER
  - Description: Adenocarcinoma is a type of bladder cancer
  - Source: ESOPHAGEAL CANCER
  - Target: ADENOCARCINOMA
  - Description: Adenocarcinoma is a subtype of esophageal cancer
  - Source: ADENOMA
  - Target: ADENOCARCINOMA
  - Description: Cancer that forms in an adenoma is called adenocarcinoma

---</data>
    </node>
