    <node id="SKIN BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Skin biopsy is a diagnostic procedure in which a lesion or a small sample of skin tissue is removed and sent to a laboratory for analysis. It is the only way to confirm squamous cell skin cancer and is an essential part of testing for this condition. A pathologist examines the removed tissue to determine whether cancer is present. Skin biopsy can also involve taking a small sample of inflamed skin on the breast, which is often used to help diagnose inflammatory breast cancer. In all cases, the procedure is performed to remove a sample of skin and test it for cancer or other abnormal changes, with the results guiding further diagnosis and treatment.
Relationships:
  - Source: BIOPSY
  - Target: SKIN BIOPSY
  - Description: Skin biopsy is a type of biopsy.
  - Source: DOCTOR
  - Target: SKIN BIOPSY
  - Description: A doctor performs a skin biopsy to diagnose squamous cell skin cancer and to inform risk assessment. The doctor selects the biopsy method based on the affected area's size, location, and other factors, and discusses the risk of scarring with the patient.
  - Source: PATHOLOGIST
  - Target: SKIN BIOPSY
  - Description: A pathologist reviews a skin biopsy sample to determine whether squamous cell skin cancer is present. By analyzing the tissue sample obtained during the skin biopsy, the pathologist identifies the specific type and subtype of cancer, providing a precise diagnosis.
  - Source: LAB
  - Target: SKIN BIOPSY
  - Description: A sample from a skin biopsy is sent to a lab to be examined under a microscope.
  - Source: SKIN BIOPSY
  - Target: EXCISION BIOPSY
  - Description: An excision biopsy is a type of skin biopsy.
  - Source: SKIN BIOPSY
  - Target: SKIN SHAVE BIOPSY
  - Description: A skin shave biopsy is a type of skin biopsy.
  - Source: SKIN BIOPSY
  - Target: SKIN PUNCH BIOPSY
  - Description: A skin punch biopsy is a type of skin biopsy.
  - Source: SKIN BIOPSY
  - Target: IMAGING SCANS
  - Description: If skin cancer goes deeper into the skin or has spread, imaging scans are done after diagnosis and biopsy</data>
