    <node id="TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Transabdominal surgery, also called abdominal surgery or transabdominal resection, is a surgical approach that involves cutting into the abdomen to remove a rectal tumor, surrounding healthy tissue, and nearby lymph nodes. After the procedure, the removed tissue and lymph nodes are sent to a laboratory for testing. This surgery is recommended for certain rectal cancer cases, including T2 tumors, some T1 tumors, and some larger T3 cancers that have not spread to lymph nodes. It is also performed after local excision when high-risk features or muscle invasion are identified. During transabdominal surgery, at least 12 lymph nodes should be removed for proper staging and evaluation.
Relationships: (No relationships listed in the data)</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">9</data>
