    <node id="ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a radical surgical procedure performed through a transabdominal approach. It is used to treat persistent, progressive, or recurrent anal cancer, as well as tumors in the lower rectum that may have grown into the anus or pelvic floor muscle. The procedure involves the removal of the anus, the rectum, the area where the rectum and colon meet (rectosigmoid junction), and surrounding fat. Additional tissues may also be removed, including levator muscles and, in some cases, groin lymph nodes. APR always requires a permanent colostomy, as the natural passage for waste elimination is removed.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: RECURRENCE
  - Target: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Description: If cancer returns to the anal area, abdominoperineal resection is recommended

  - Source: PROGRESSIVE DISEASE
  - Target: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Description: Surgery to remove the anus is recommended for locally progressive anal cancer

  - Source: COLOSTOMY
  - Target: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Description: Abdominoperineal resection always requires a permanent colostomy

  - Source: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Target: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Description: Abdominoperineal resection is a type of transabdominal surgery used for tumors in the lower rectum

  - Source: LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)
  - Target: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Description: Both are types of transabdominal surgery for rectal tumors, but LAR is used for mid/upper rectum and APR for lower rectum

  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Target: GROIN DISSECTION
  - Description: A groin dissection is performed during APR if groin lymph nodes are known to contain cancer

  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Target: MUSCLE FLAP RECONSTRUCTION
  - Description: Muscle flap reconstruction can improve healing of the perineum and reduce complications after APR

  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Target: PERMANENT COLOSTOMY
  - Description: APR requires creating a permanent colostomy because the anus is removed</data>
