    <node id="- SOURCE: CLL SPECIALIST">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The CLL specialist is involved in the patient's CLL care and shares patient-management responsibilities with the broader cancer care team.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">  - Target: CANCER CARE TEAM
  - Description: The CLL specialist is involved in the patient's CLL care and shares patient-management responsibilities with the broader cancer care team.

ENTITY: CARE TEAM
Description: The care team, also referred to as the health care team, is the group of healthcare professionals involved in a patient’s care, particularly during diagnosis, treatment, and ongoing support. This team is central to cancer care and may include doctors, nurses, physicians, pharmacists, social workers, and other specialists, such as gynecologic or oncology care teams. The care team coordinates care and works together to provide treatment options, manage side effects, and support the patient throughout their healthcare journey.

The care team is responsible for performing and reviewing tests, prescribing medicines, and putting together all test results—for example, to determine whether prostate cancer is lower risk or higher risk. They also guide treatment planning, arrange additional tests such as genetic testing, and help patients make informed decisions about their care. Beyond medical treatment, the care team provides psychosocial support and can address a range of concerns, including genetic counseling, quitting smoking, and clinical trial eligibility.

Patients are strongly encouraged to actively engage with their care team. They should talk to the team about any claustrophobia before an MRI, as well as any allergies to contrast material. Patients are also advised to ask questions, seek support, and share important health information, including symptoms and changes in medications. It is recommended that patients get to know their care team, keep their contact information handy, and communicate with them regularly—often through tools like MyChart—throughout diagnosis and treatment.

In summary, the care team is a collaborative group of healthcare providers who work with the patient to ensure comprehensive, coordinated care. They answer questions, explain treatment options, prescribe medicines, review test results, and manage side effects, all while addressing the patient’s physical, emotional, and practical needs. By maintaining open communication and involving the care team in every step, patients can receive more personalized and effective care.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: FERTILITY AND FAMILY PLANNING
Description: Fertility and family planning is an important supportive care topic for cancer patients, addressing the reproductive health challenges that can arise from cancer treatment. Specifically, fertility and family planning counseling is designed to help individuals understand the fertility-related risks associated with cancer therapies and explore options for preserving fertility. This is particularly relevant for young adults diagnosed with colon cancer, as treatments may impact their ability to conceive or bear children in the future. The counseling aims to provide education and guidance on fertility preservation methods, allowing patients to make informed decisions before, during, and after treatment.

In addition to its role for colon cancer patients, fertility and family planning is also recognized as a key topic in patient education materials for people with anal cancer. The inclusion of this subject in patient guides for anal cancer underscores its broad applicability across different cancer types, ensuring that all patients have access to information about how their treatment may affect fertility and what family planning options are available.

Overall, fertility and family planning encompasses both medical counseling and informational resources, helping cancer patients navigate the complex intersection of oncology and reproductive health. By integrating this topic into cancer care, healthcare providers can better support patients' long-term quality of life and reproductive autonomy.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: COUNSELING
Description: A discussion about fertility preservation options that patients may be referred to before cancer treatment
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: ONCOLOGY NURSE
Description: ONCOLOGY NURSE is a specialized nursing professional who works in cancer care. Oncology nurses provide hands-on care to patients, including administering systemic therapies such as chemotherapy, managing overall patient care, answering questions, and helping patients cope with treatment side effects. They are sometimes referred to as nurse navigators, guiding patients through the complexities of cancer treatment. Additionally, oncology nurses may offer counseling to patients who are at genetic risk of developing skin cancer, supporting prevention and early detection efforts. This role combines clinical expertise, patient education, and emotional support across the cancer care continuum.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: FERTILITY COUNSELING
Description: FERTILITY COUNSELING is a consultation process provided to individuals of all genders as part of overall treatment planning. It involves education and discussion about fertility preservation options and the potential fertility-related risks associated with a patient’s proposed medical treatment. This counseling ensures that patients are informed about how their treatment may affect their reproductive health and what steps can be taken to preserve fertility for the future.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: FERTILITY
Description: Fertility (all genders) is a topic covered before cancer treatment because of the potential impact of treatment on fertility.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM (MDT)
Description: The multidisciplinary team is a team of health care and psychosocial care professionals from different professional backgrounds with expertise in the patient's type of cancer.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: ONCOLOGIST
Description: A physician who specializes in cancer and may provide cancer genetics counseling during treatment.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST
Description: MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST is a physician who specializes in the diagnosis and treatment of cancer through the use of systemic drug therapy. As a key member of the cancer care team, a medical oncologist treats cancer using medications that may be administered by mouth, injection, or intravenously. This specialist is specifically trained to prescribe and manage cancer-treatment drugs, including chemotherapy, and plays a central role in coordinating a patient's overall cancer treatment plan.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM
Description: A multidisciplinary team (MDT) is a group of health care and psychosocial care professionals from diverse professional backgrounds who possess specialized knowledge and experience in the patient's specific type of cancer. These experts are united in their purpose of collaboratively planning and implementing the patient's treatment, ensuring a comprehensive and coordinated approach to cancer care.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: DOCTOR
Description: The doctor is a physician and healthcare professional who serves as a key member of the patient's care team. In this role, they conduct comprehensive assessments, including asking about symptoms, performing physical exams (such as skin exams, lymph node examinations, and digital rectal exams), and reviewing the patient’s family health history of blood relatives to help determine the best treatment approach. The doctor evaluates the patient’s cancer risk, reviews prior surgery and biopsy results, and guides treatment decisions. They also review pathology reports with the patient, may suggest genetic testing, and can refer the patient to a genetic counselor.

The doctor is responsible for discussing test results—including imaging, laboratory, and blood or biochemical tests—with the patient, receiving and interpreting these results, and ordering additional tests as needed. They may order imaging tests during follow-up after cancer treatment. In addition, the doctor selects the biopsy method, discusses biopsy risks and scarring, and may order imaging tests. For confirmed squamous cell skin cancer, the doctor evaluates features to determine whether the cancer is low-, high-, or very high-risk and considers appropriate treatment options.

Beyond diagnosis and treatment planning, the doctor addresses broader patient concerns. They are consulted about birth control options, pregnancy planning, and other treatment-related concerns, and they may prescribe treatments such as iron supplements. The doctor also explains medical information clearly; patients are encouraged to ask for simpler terms when needed. Throughout the care journey, the doctor is a central contact with whom the patient discusses test results and attends visits, ensuring continuity of care and coordination of next steps.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: FERTILITY PRESERVATION
Description: FERTILITY PRESERVATION is the process of keeping options open for having biological children in the future, typically considered and undertaken before starting cancer treatment. It involves specific actions taken prior to cancer therapy to preserve a person’s ability to have children later. This may require a referral to a fertility specialist before treatment begins, so that suitable preservation methods can be discussed and initiated. Available options may include sperm banking for males, as well as counseling and other fertility preservation techniques tailored to the individual’s situation. The goal is to give patients the opportunity to have biological children after cancer treatment. Organizations such as the NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) provide information and guidance on fertility preservation. Overall, fertility preservation is a proactive step that helps maintain future reproductive possibilities for cancer patients.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: MEDICAL PROVIDERS
Description: Healthcare professionals and institutions that can discuss family cancer history and genetic testing with a patient.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: MELISSA
Description: Melissa is an anal cancer survivor quoted in the text; she advises choosing an oncology team experienced in treating anal cancer.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: GENETIC COUNSELOR
Description: A genetic counselor is a health professional with specialized training in genetic diseases and cancer genetics. They help patients understand gene changes related to disease and hereditary cancer risk, and assist individuals in deciding whether to undergo germline testing. In addition, genetic counselors interpret the results of genetic tests, providing expert guidance and counseling. They may also provide counseling as recommended by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) for people with a genetic risk of skin cancer. Overall, genetic counselors support patients in making informed decisions about genetic testing and understanding the implications of their genetic test results.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: SURGEON
Description: SURGEON is a medical professional who specializes in performing surgical procedures. As a physician, the surgeon may also provide cancer genetics counseling and perform diagnostic procedures such as skin biopsies. In the context of cancer care, the surgeon is skilled in identifying and locating sentinel lymph nodes, which are key to determining whether cancer has spread. Thus, the surgeon’s role combines operative expertise with diagnostic and counseling responsibilities in patient care.
Relationships: None listed in the data.

ENTITY: NURSE NAVIGATOR
Description: Nurse navigator is another name for an oncology nurse who helps guide patients through cancer care, providing education, coordination, and support.
Relationships: None listed in the data.
</data>
