    <edge source="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN Cancer Centers and the U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) are both recognized resources for locating clinical trials. NCCN Cancer Centers are specifically listed as a resource for finding clinical trials within the United States, while the NLM provides the worldwide clinicaltrials.gov database. Together, these two entities offer complementary access to clinical trial information: NCCN Cancer Centers focus on U.S.-based cancer centers, and the NLM supplies a comprehensive, global database of clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" target="NCCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN is the network associated with NCCN Cancer Centers.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN Cancer Centers is listed as a resource for finding clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN Cancer Centers serve as a key resource for identifying and accessing clinical trials. These centers are formally recognized as a U.S.-based resource for individuals seeking information about clinical trial opportunities. The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) Cancer Centers provide a curated listing that helps patients, caregivers, and healthcare professionals locate relevant clinical trials across the United States. By utilizing these centers, users can connect with ongoing research studies and potentially participate in the advancement of cancer treatment. The entity "NCCN CANCER CENTERS" is thus directly associated with the facilitation of "CLINICAL TRIAL" discovery. Together, the descriptions emphasize that NCCN Cancer Centers are a reliable, U.S.-specific source for finding clinical trials, offering a centralized approach to navigating the complex landscape of cancer research.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" target="UNITED STATES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN Cancer Centers are a network of cancer treatment and research institutions located throughout the United States. These centers are recognized as a key resource for patients and providers seeking information about clinical trials. Specifically, the NCCN Cancer Centers are listed under the category "In the United States" as a resource for finding clinical trials. This listing indicates that the centers serve as a designated point of reference for individuals looking to identify and access clinical trial opportunities within the country. The descriptions consistently emphasize that NCCN Cancer Centers are based in the United States and function as a practical tool for clinical trial discovery, making the information accessible and relevant to a U.S. audience. In summary, NCCN Cancer Centers comprise a U.S.-based network, and they are formally indexed as a clinical trial finding resource within the United States, ensuring that patients and clinicians can readily locate trials through these centers.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" target="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) and NCCN Cancer Centers are both U.S. organizations listed as resources for finding cancer clinical trials. They serve as authoritative sources for locating clinical trials available in the United States. NCCN Cancer Centers can be accessed online at NCCN.org/cancercenters, while the NCI's corresponding resource is available at cancer.gov. Both are recognized for providing information on cancer clinical trials to patients, caregivers, and researchers.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CDC" target="CDC.GOV/TOBACCO" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) operates the online resource CDC.gov/tobacco, which serves as the agency's dedicated digital platform for tobacco cessation. As the CDC's official online tobacco cessation resource, CDC.gov/tobacco provides information and support to help individuals quit tobacco use, reflecting the CDC's broader public health mission to reduce tobacco-related disease and death. This platform is a key component of the CDC's efforts to offer accessible cessation guidance and resources to the public.</data>
      <data key="d8">CDC</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CDC" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CDC.gov/tobacco provides smoking cessation resources; quitting smoking/vaping is important for effective cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CDC</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="QUESTIONS TO ASK" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Questions to Ask section helps patients ask their care team questions</data>
      <data key="d8">QUESTIONS TO ASK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="QUESTIONS TO ASK" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The suggested questions are directed to the patient's care team</data>
      <data key="d8">QUESTIONS TO ASK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENTS" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient has received treatments and should note each treatment and its start date.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="MEDICAL PROVIDERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Medical providers are part of the patient's care team.</data>
      <data key="d8">MEDICAL PROVIDERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="FAMILY MEMBERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team asks about the health history of family members who are blood relatives.</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY MEMBERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team is a collaborative group that includes health care providers as integral members. Health care providers are part of the care team, serving as members who contribute to the team's overall function. In essence, the health care provider is a constituent of the care team, with both descriptions emphasizing that providers belong to and participate within the care team structure.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="TREATMENTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team may prescribe medicines and determine which treatment is best for the patient.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The CARE TEAM and the PATIENT work together as partners in the patient's healthcare journey. The care team is responsible for assembling and reviewing test results, using this information to manage and coordinate the patient's ongoing care. In this collaborative relationship, the patient actively participates by discussing test results with the care team and following the recommendations provided. The patient is encouraged to communicate openly with the care team, share symptoms and concerns, and help the care team get to know them as an individual. In return, the care team provides care, guidance, and management based on the patient's unique needs. Together, the CARE TEAM and the PATIENT form a cooperative unit focused on achieving the best possible health outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team can be consulted to determine whether a patient qualifies for an available clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="RESEARCH TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The research team is part of the broader care team for patients in a clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d8">RESEARCH TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="FAMILY HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team asks about the health history of blood relatives (family history)</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="IMAGING STUDIES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team orders and interprets imaging studies as part of diagnosis and treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING STUDIES</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team helps manage side effects such as nausea and vomiting during chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team orders and uses biopsy results as part of diagnosis and treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team plans treatment and needs to know the stage and grade of the cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="DOCTOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The doctor is a member of the patient's care team. The care team includes the doctor as part of the patient's care team. Therefore, the doctor and the care team are connected: the doctor participates in the care team, which is responsible for the patient's care.</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="GENETIC COUNSELOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team can help a patient access a genetic counselor</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC COUNSELOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="GENETIC COUNSELING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text tells patients to ask their care team about genetic counseling</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC COUNSELING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="GENETIC TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team focuses on assessing cancer risk by inquiring about family history and the use of genetic testing. Specifically, they ask whether family members have undergone genetic testing, as this information helps clarify hereditary risk and guide patient care.</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="MEDICAL HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team uses the patient's medical history to help determine which treatment is best. By reviewing the medical history, the care team can assess relevant health information and make informed decisions about the most appropriate course of treatment for the patient.</data>
      <data key="d8">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="VAPING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team can help a patient quit vaping through counseling and medicines.</data>
      <data key="d8">VAPING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="QUITTING SMOKING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team can provide counseling and medicines to help a patient quit smoking or vaping.</data>
      <data key="d8">QUITTING SMOKING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The treatment team screens the patient's level of distress and provides cancer care</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="FAMILY MEMBERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Family members can provide transportation, meals, emotional support, and note-taking for the patient</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY MEMBERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="NCCN GUIDELINES FOR PATIENTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The guidelines are written for patients and are trusted by them</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN GUIDELINES FOR PATIENTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient should discuss imaging test results with the health care provider</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient undergoes cancer treatment, which may affect fertility, hearing, and other functions.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="DOCTOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient and the doctor are engaged in an ongoing medical consultation. During their visits, the patient actively asks questions and discusses test results with the doctor. The patient also seeks guidance on birth control options and consults the doctor about the best time to plan a pregnancy. Through these interactions, the patient works with the doctor to address reproductive health concerns and make informed decisions.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient may undergo a biopsy to help diagnose and treat the condition</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy can affect the patient's fertility</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="IMAGING TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging tests are used to show the primary tumor and look for cancer in other parts of the patient's body</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="BLOOD TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient may undergo blood tests to help diagnose and treat the condition</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="MEDICAL HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient provides a medical history to help determine the best treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="FERTILITY PRESERVATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">To preserve fertility, the patient may need to take action before starting cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="HEARING TEST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A hearing test can assess hearing loss that may be caused by tumors or treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="PREGNANCY TEST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Those who can become pregnant will have a pregnancy test before starting treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="NICOTINE WITHDRAWAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The stress of having cancer may make it harder for the patient to quit and manage nicotine withdrawal</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="DOCTOR VISITS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Patients attend doctor visits and are encouraged to bring someone for support</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="APPOINTMENTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Patients are encouraged to track appointments through a patient portal</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="EYE EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">An eye exam might be recommended for the patient before starting treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="NUTRITION ASSESSMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient may have a nutrition assessment to receive advice on the best foods and fluids before treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient updates the primary care physician about any changes in health, treatment, and dates</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="TELEHEALTH VISITS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Patients may have a friend or family member join telehealth visits</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="FERTILITY SPECIALIST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient should be referred to a fertility specialist to discuss options before starting treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="SMOKING OR VAPING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If the patient smokes or vapes, they should seek help to quit</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="SPEECH AND SWALLOWING ASSESSMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient's ability to swallow and speak is tested before and after treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="TISSUE TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient may undergo tissue tests to help diagnose and treat the condition</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="IMAGING STUDIES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient may undergo imaging studies to help diagnose and treat the condition</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="FRIENDS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Friends can provide transportation, meals, and emotional support to the patient</data>
      <data key="d8">FRIENDS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="PEERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Peers can be part of the patient's support system</data>
      <data key="d8">PEERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="NUTRITION EXPERT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient may meet with a nutrition expert before starting treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="SPEECH AND SWALLOWING THERAPIST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient may visit a speech or swallowing therapist to test swallowing and speaking before and after treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="REPRODUCTIVE SPECIALIST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Fertility and reproductive specialists help the patient sort through fertility options</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="CAREGIVER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Caregivers support the patient and should also ask questions during appointments</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATIENT" target="CANCER CARE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cancer care is provided to the patient and involves shared decision-making</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="FAMILY MEMBERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The doctor asks about the health history of family members who are blood relatives</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY MEMBERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A doctor is a type of health care provider</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Dr. [Doctor] provides ongoing care for the patient, including performing skin exams as part of their medical treatment. The patient, in turn, actively participates in their care by asking the doctor to review their pathology report. Together, these interactions reflect a collaborative doctor-patient relationship focused on evaluation, diagnosis, and informed treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="GENETIC TEST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The doctor can talk to the patient about genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="IMAGING TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The doctor may order imaging tests during follow-up to check for cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Doctor is a member of the patient's care team. As part of this team, the doctor works collaboratively with other care team members to plan and manage the patient's care.</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="GERMLINE TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A doctor might suggest genetic germline testing if family history suggests inherited cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d8">GERMLINE TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="GENETIC COUNSELOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A doctor can refer a patient to a genetic counselor.</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC COUNSELOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="GENETIC TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The doctor asks whether family members had genetic testing</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="SKIN BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A doctor performs a skin biopsy to diagnose squamous cell skin cancer and to inform risk assessment. The doctor selects the biopsy method based on the affected area's size, location, and other factors, and discusses the risk of scarring with the patient.</data>
      <data key="d8">SKIN BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The treatment team reviews prior biopsy results and tumor tissue</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biopsy samples are examined to look for cancer cells</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biopsy results and biopsy findings play a central role in guiding cancer treatment decisions. After a cancer diagnosis, these results are used to help plan the appropriate treatment approach. Additionally, biopsy findings provide specific information about which treatments the cancer may respond to, enabling more targeted and effective therapy. Together, biopsy and treatment are directly connected: the biopsy supplies essential diagnostic and predictive data, while treatment planning relies on that data to determine the best course of action for each patient.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="TREATMENT PLANNING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biopsy results are used to determine the treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT PLANNING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient undergoes a biopsy to evaluate for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If chemotherapy is planned first, a biopsy is performed to remove a sample of the tumor before treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team commonly requests a biopsy sample to confirm a diagnosis of glioma. In addition, the care team uses the results from that biopsy to help determine the patient's cancer risk. Together, the biopsy and the care team are closely connected: the biopsy provides essential diagnostic information, while the care team relies on this information both to confirm the presence of glioma and to assess the overall risk associated with the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="BIOMARKER TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biopsy and biomarker testing are closely related procedures in the diagnosis and treatment of cancer. A biopsy involves obtaining a sample of tumor tissue from the patient. This biopsy sample is then used for biomarker testing, which analyzes the tumor for specific molecular components, such as genes, proteins, or other biomarkers. In other words, biomarker testing relies on a tissue sample obtained through biopsy, allowing healthcare providers to identify molecular characteristics of the tumor that may guide treatment decisions. Together, biopsy and biomarker testing enable a detailed understanding of the tumor's biology.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A biopsy sample may provide the diagnosis of anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="GENETIC TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing can be performed on tumor tissue, and biopsy provides tissue samples for such testing.</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="MEDICAL PROCEDURE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biopsy is a medical procedure.</data>
      <data key="d8">MEDICAL PROCEDURE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="TUMOR STAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Tumor staging is based on imaging scans, biopsies, and blood tests</data>
      <data key="d8">TUMOR STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="GLEASON SCORE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biopsy samples are used to calculate the Gleason score via Gleason patterns.</data>
      <data key="d8">GLEASON SCORE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A biopsy is key to confirming the diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A biopsy is a medical procedure in which a sample of tissue is taken from the body for examination, and it is required to diagnose ovarian cancer. This procedure is performed not only to confirm the presence of ovarian cancer but also to determine its specific type and grade, which are essential for guiding treatment decisions. Thus, the biopsy serves as the definitive diagnostic tool for ovarian cancer, providing critical information about the disease's characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="MOLECULAR BIOMARKER TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Molecular biomarker testing uses a sample from a biopsy to examine its molecular components.</data>
      <data key="d8">MOLECULAR BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="LYMPH NODE BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Lymph node biopsy is a type of biopsy</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CT SCAN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scan may be used to guide a lymph node biopsy</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="ULTRASOUND" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ultrasound plays a key role in biopsy procedures, serving both as a guidance tool and as a diagnostic aid. During a biopsy, ultrasound can be used to guide the needle to the precise location of a target tissue, improving accuracy and safety. This guidance is especially useful for lymph node biopsies, where ultrasound helps direct the procedure to the affected node. Additionally, ultrasound results help determine whether a needle biopsy is needed for a thyroid nodule, as imaging findings can indicate whether sampling is necessary. Thus, ultrasound supports biopsy decision-making and execution across different clinical contexts.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biopsy and surgery are two closely related medical procedures that both involve the removal of biological material for pathological examination. In both biopsy and surgery, samples of tissue are collected and sent to a pathology laboratory for analysis. Pathologists are the specialists responsible for examining the cells, tissues, and organs removed during either a biopsy or a surgical procedure, providing crucial diagnostic information. Biopsy is typically performed as part of the initial surgical intervention to remove cancer. When a patient undergoes surgery for cancer, the tumor and other affected tissues removed during the operation are routinely tested, and this removed tissue effectively serves as biopsy material for pathological evaluation. In this way, surgery can provide the necessary biopsy specimens even when a separate, dedicated biopsy procedure was not performed beforehand. Additionally, the results from any prior surgery and earlier biopsy procedures are often reviewed to inform treatment decisions and to compare with current findings. The relationship between the two procedures is complementary: a biopsy can be a standalone, minimally invasive procedure used to obtain a small sample for diagnosis, or it can be integrated into a larger surgical operation where the entire tumor or organ is removed and subsequently examined. When surgery is planned as the first step, the tissues excised during the operation are submitted for testing, fulfilling the role of a biopsy. Conversely, when a biopsy is performed first, its results help guide the surgical plan. Ultimately, both biopsy and surgery are essential sources of tissue specimens, and the pathologist's analysis of these specimens forms the foundation for accurate diagnosis, staging, and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging results, combined with PSA and exam findings, help doctors decide whether a biopsy is needed</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="COMPLICATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Having a biopsy may cause complications such as infection, bleeding, or blood in the urine, stool, or semen.</data>
      <data key="d8">COMPLICATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A biopsy can be taken from a metastasis, for example in the liver, using a hollow needle</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A biopsy is a medical procedure used to confirm a diagnosis of prostate cancer. It is the definitive method for diagnosing the disease, as it allows healthcare providers to obtain tissue samples from the prostate gland for pathological examination. In the context of prostate cancer, a biopsy serves to verify the presence of cancerous cells, thereby confirming or ruling out the condition. Both descriptions agree that the primary role of a biopsy is diagnostic confirmation. Therefore, when prostate cancer is suspectedbased on elevated prostate-specific antigen (PSA) levels, abnormal digital rectal exam findings, or imaging resultsa biopsy is the essential next step to establish a definitive diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="DIAGNOSIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A biopsy is a diagnostic procedure that plays a central role in determining a patient's diagnosis. It is specifically used to establish whether a tumor or lesion is benign or malignant, thereby helping to determine tumor status. By collecting and examining tissue samples, a biopsy provides critical information that supports and confirms a diagnosis, particularly in cases of suspected cancer. The results obtained from a biopsy are directly used in the diagnostic process, enabling clinicians to make informed decisions about the nature of the disease and subsequent treatment. In summary, biopsy is an essential diagnostic tool, and its findings are integral to the overall diagnosis, especially for confirming cancer and assessing tumor status.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
