    <edge source="SURGERY" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The treatment team reviews prior surgery and testing results</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery is a type of treatment, specifically a form of cancer treatment. It is one of the main treatment options available for cancer, including cervical cancer, where it is recognized as a primary treatment approach. Surgery can also be combined with other therapies; when drug therapy is administered before surgery, the pathologic stage of the disease may include a "y" prefix to indicate this sequence. As a treatment modality, surgery plays a key role in the management of cancer and is included among the main types of treatment for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">SURGERY and CLINICAL TRIALS are both recognized treatment approaches in oncology, applicable to different cancer types. According to the provided descriptions, surgery is a treatment option for thyroid cancer, and clinical trials are also listed as another treatment option for the same disease. Similarly, both surgery and clinical trials are identified as treatment options for squamous cell skin cancer. Thus, these two entities are consistently presented as parallel therapeutic avenues for both thyroid cancer and squamous cell skin cancer. No contradictions exist between the descriptions. The combined understanding is that SURGERY and CLINICAL TRIALS are each considered viable treatment modalities for thyroid cancer and squamous cell skin cancer, with clinical trials offering access to investigational or alternative approaches alongside standard surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="SMOKING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Smoking greatly increases the chance of side effects during and after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d8">SMOKING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">SURGERY and CHEMOTHERAPY are two primary treatment modalities used for ovarian cancer, and a gynecologic oncologist is an expert in both. These treatments are often combined in a coordinated sequence, and the order in which they are administered depends on the extent of the cancer at diagnosis.

CHEMOTHERAPY may be given before SURGERY, in which case it is referred to as neoadjuvant therapy. This approach is typically used when the cancer has spread too extensively to be removed safely or completely by initial SURGERY. By shrinking the tumors first, neoadjuvant chemotherapy can make subsequent surgical removal more feasible and effective. Alternatively, CHEMOTHERAPY may be given after SURGERY, known as primary or adjuvant chemotherapy. This is used to eliminate any remaining cancer cells that may not have been visible or removable during the operation. Adjuvant chemotherapy is recommended after SURGERY for most stage 1 ovarian cancers, as well as for stages 2, 3, and 4.

In summary, SURGERY and CHEMOTHERAPY are both essential and complementary tools in treating ovarian cancer. Their optimal sequence is determined by disease stage and resectability: if SURGERY cannot be performed first due to extensive spread, CHEMOTHERAPY is administered before SURGERY; otherwise, SURGERY is performed first and followed by CHEMOTHERAPY. The combined expertise of a gynecologic oncologist covers both modalities, ensuring that patients receive the most appropriate and individualized treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="NEUROSURGEON" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The neurosurgeon performs surgery in the operating room to remove tumor tissue.</data>
      <data key="d8">NEUROSURGEON</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery is a primary and often initial treatment for ovarian cancer. It serves two key purposes: removing the cancerous tissue and determining the extent of the disease, a process known as staging. By surgically removing ovarian cancer, doctors aim to eliminate as much of the tumor as possible, which is essential for effective treatment. Additionally, the information gathered during surgery helps assess how far the cancer has spread, guiding subsequent treatment decisions. Thus, surgery plays a dual role in both treating and evaluating ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
