    <edge source="NCCN" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) provides guidance and resources regarding clinical trials across multiple cancer types. For primary CNS lymphoma, NCCN experts recommend that all patients consider joining a clinical trial as part of their treatment approach. NCCN offers patient guidelines and cancer center information designed to help patients find and understand clinical trials. Additionally, NCCN's patient education materials list clinical trials as a treatment option for bladder cancer, noting that patients can join a clinical trial at any time during their care. Through these various resources, NCCN highlights clinical trials as an accessible and important treatment pathway for patients.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN patient guidelines cover clinical trials and encourage patients to ask their care team about access to clinical trials. NCCN patient guide provides information about cancer care and encourages patients to consider asking their care team about access to clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) and the U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) are both recognized resources for locating clinical trials. NCCN provides clinical trial resources specifically for the United States, primarily through its network of NCCN Cancer Centers. In contrast, the U.S. National Library of Medicine offers worldwide clinical trial resources, most notably through the ClinicalTrials.gov database. Together, they serve as complementary tools: NCCN focuses on U.S.-based trial options, while NLM covers global trial listings.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides supportive-care information and patient guidelines that are directly relevant to individuals undergoing chemotherapy. In its patient education materials, the NCCN describes chemotherapy as a type of systemic therapy, meaning it travels through the bloodstream to treat cancer cells throughout the body. Together, these descriptions highlight the NCCNs dual role in both educating patients about the nature of chemotherapy and offering practical guidance for managing treatment-related care.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
