    <edge source="NCCN" target="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) develops patient guidelines that reference, describe, and apply the cancer staging system created by the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC). In its patient education materials, the NCCN uses the AJCC staging system as the foundational framework for explaining how cancer is staged. For instance, NCCNs mouth cancer staging patient information describes and explains the AJCC staging system in detail. Similarly, NCCNs patient guidelines on throat cancer staging explain and apply the same AJCC staging system, with a dedicated chapter on throat cancer staging that relies on the AJCC framework. Across these various cancer-specific resources, the NCCN consistently incorporates the AJCC staging system to help patients understand the extent of their disease, prognosis, and treatment planning. Thus, the NCCNs patient-facing guidelines are aligned with the authoritative staging definitions and criteria established by the American Joint Committee on Cancer, ensuring that patients receive standardized, evidence-based information about cancer staging.</data>
      <data key="d8">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
