    <edge source="NCCN" target="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN is an organization that publishes clinical guidelines and provides resources related to cancer care. Among its resources is the NCCN Cancer Centers network, which consists of cancer centers affiliated with NCCN. These NCCN Cancer Centers are listed on the NCCN website, specifically at NCCN.org/cancercenters, and serve as a clinical trial resource. The list is designed to help patients and providers find cancer centers and available clinical trials in the United States. NCCN Cancer Centers are therefore an integral part of NCCN's effort to connect individuals with cancer treatment and research opportunities, while NCCN itself is recognized for its clinical guidelines and its role in maintaining this network of affiliated cancer centers.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="THE ANCHOR STUDY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Anchor Study is listed as a resource in the NCCN Guidelines for Patients.</data>
      <data key="d8">THE ANCHOR STUDY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="FERTILITY PRESERVATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides accessible information on fertility preservation, particularly for adolescents and young adults affected by cancer. This guidance is available through multiple platforms, including the official NCCN.org website and the NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app. NCCNs patient guidelines and the mobile app are key resources that offer detailed information on fertility preservation for cancer patients, helping individuals understand their options before, during, and after treatment. The organization emphasizes fertility preservation as an important aspect of cancer care, ensuring that patients and families have the knowledge needed to make informed decisions.</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY PRESERVATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The NCCN patient guide focuses on squamous cell carcinoma, the most common type of nasopharyngeal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="SQUAMOUS CELL SKIN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides patient-friendly guidelines for squamous cell skin cancer. These guidelines are accessible through the NCCN website at NCCN.org/patientguidelines and through the NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer mobile application. The materials direct readers to the NCCN Guidelines for Patients as a comprehensive source of information on squamous cell skin cancer, offering guidance on diagnosis, treatment, and supportive care. The NCCN thus serves as a key resource for patients seeking reliable, expert-reviewed information about this condition.</data>
      <data key="d8">SQUAMOUS CELL SKIN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
