      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy regimens containing irinotecan tend to cause diarrhea</data>
      <data key="d8">DIARRHEA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="TARGETED THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Targeted therapy can be combined with chemotherapy, and may have fewer side effects than chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy and radiation therapy are two treatment modalities that are frequently combined to enhance effectiveness, particularly in oncology. When used together, the combination is referred to as chemoradiation. Chemotherapy may be administered as part of recommended treatment regimens alongside radiation therapy to treat anal cancer. Additionally, chemotherapy may be given in addition to radiation for recurrent inguinal lymph nodes, with chemoradiation combining both modalities. Radiation therapy, for its part, may be delivered alone or in combination with chemotherapy, and can also be combined with systemic therapy in certain situations. In all cases, the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapytermed chemoradiationrepresents a coordinated approach in which the two treatments complement each other, whether the goal is curative treatment for anal cancer or management of recurrent disease in inguinal lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A biopsy is needed to confirm ovarian cancer before chemotherapy is started</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team talks with the patient about the types of chemotherapy, doses, timing, and side effects</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy regimens are recommended for use with radiation therapy to treat stage I, II, and III anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The clinical trial could involve treatment with chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic anal cancer is treated with platinum-based chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CHEMORADIATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a cancer treatment that uses drugs to kill or slow the growth of cancer cells. Chemoradiation is a treatment approach that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. In this combined regimen, chemotherapy serves as one of the core components, specifically functioning as the chemotherapy portion of the treatment. Chemoradiation may be followed by additional chemotherapy, which is given to kill any lingering cancer cells that remain after the initial combined treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is one of the main treatments for ovarian cancer. In cases where ovarian cancer has spread too extensively for initial surgery, chemotherapy is needed first.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment and one component of the broader category of cancer treatment. It is used to combat cancer but can have side effects, notably affecting fertility. Thus, while chemotherapy is a key part of cancer treatment, it is not the entirety of it, and its impact on fertility is an important consideration for patients.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a form of systemic therapy. Systemic therapy is a treatment approach that uses substances that travel through the bloodstream, reaching and affecting cells throughout the entire body rather than targeting a single, localized area. Chemotherapy specifically involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy rapidly dividing cells, most commonly cancer cells, by interfering with their ability to grow and multiply. Because chemotherapy is a systemic therapy, it can treat cancer that has spread to multiple sites in the body, unlike localized treatments such as surgery or radiation therapy, which focus on a specific region. In summary, chemotherapy and systemic therapy are closely related: chemotherapy is one specific type of systemic therapy, and systemic therapy is the broader category encompassing treatments that work throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a main treatment for primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="SUPPORTIVE CARE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supportive care is a specialized area of healthcare focused on relieving the side effects and symptoms caused by cancer treatment, particularly chemotherapy. It is used to manage the physical and emotional challenges that may arise during treatment, helping patients maintain their quality of life. While chemotherapy is the primary treatment aimed at targeting cancer cells, supportive care works alongside it to address the adverse effects it can cause, such as nausea, fatigue, pain, and other treatment-related complications. In this way, supportive care plays a crucial complementary role in the overall management of cancer patients, ensuring that the side effects of chemotherapy are minimized and that patients are better able to tolerate and continue their treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="MITOMYCIN AND CAPECITABINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Mitomycin and capecitabine is a chemotherapy regimen</data>
      <data key="d8">MITOMYCIN AND CAPECITABINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="FERTILITY PRESERVATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy can have a detrimental effect on fertility, as the treatment may harm reproductive organs or function. Because of this potential impact, patients who are scheduled to undergo chemotherapy may need to consider fertility preservation options before starting their treatment. Fertility preservation, therefore, becomes an important proactive step for individuals who wish to protect their ability to have biological children in the future after completing chemotherapy. This process should ideally be addressed prior to the initiation of chemotherapy, as the treatment itself can compromise fertility, making advance planning essential.</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY PRESERVATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="HAND-FOOT SYNDROME" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hand-foot syndrome is a side effect of chemotherapy, especially the chemotherapy drug capecitabine.</data>
      <data key="d8">HAND-FOOT SYNDROME</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="NEUROPATHY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy regimens containing oxaliplatin can cause nerve damage (neuropathy).</data>
      <data key="d8">NEUROPATHY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="DCF" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">DCF is a chemotherapy regimen option for metastatic anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">DCF</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="FOLFCIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">FOLFCIS is a chemotherapy regimen option for metastatic anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">FOLFCIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Patients should talk to their treatment team about the benefits of radiation therapy compared with the risk of nervous system damage</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy uses high-energy x-rays to kill cancer cells</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a type of treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="FATIGUE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Fatigue may begin after radiation therapy has finished and last for several weeks</data>
      <data key="d8">FATIGUE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="NAUSEA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Nausea is a common side effect of radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d8">NAUSEA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy and chemotherapy are two cancer treatment modalities that are often used together or compared. Chemotherapy is sometimes added to radiation therapy to enhance the overall treatment effect. In particular, chemotherapy regimens are recommended for use in combination with radiation therapy to treat stage I, II, and III anal cancer. When the two are compared as standalone treatment options, radiation therapy may be more likely than chemotherapy to damage the nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is typically given Monday through Friday for 5 to 6 weeks as part of chemoradiation for anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="ANAL CANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a component of chemoradiation for non-metastatic anal canal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="PALLIATIVE RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">RADIATION THERAPY is a broad medical treatment modality that uses ionizing radiation to treat various conditions, most notably cancer. PALLIATIVE RADIATION THERAPY is a specific subtype of radiation therapy. It is defined as a type of radiation therapy used to control symptoms, rather than to cure the underlying disease. Palliative radiation therapy aims to relieve pain, reduce pressure, or address other distressing symptoms caused by tumors or malignancies, thereby improving the patient's quality of life. As a form of radiation therapy, it shares the same fundamental principles and techniques, but its primary goal is symptom management and supportive care in advanced or incurable disease settings. Together, these descriptions establish that palliative radiation therapy is a specialized application of radiation therapy focused on alleviating symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d8">PALLIATIVE RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="SUPPORTIVE CARE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supportive care is used to relieve side effects caused by radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a treatment used for primary CNS lymphoma, a cancer that affects the central nervous system. It is considered a main treatment option for this condition, playing a key role in managing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
