      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">WBRT is a form of radiation therapy that treats the whole brain</data>
      <data key="d8">WBRT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="WHOLE-BRAIN RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Whole-brain radiation therapy is a type of radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">WHOLE-BRAIN RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="MITOMYCIN AND CAPECITABINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Capecitabine is taken on radiation days only while radiation therapy is given</data>
      <data key="d8">MITOMYCIN AND CAPECITABINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="FERTILITY PRESERVATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy can damage the ovaries and testicles and cause infertility, so fertility preservation may be considered before treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY PRESERVATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="SPERM BANKING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy may damage testicular function, and sperm banking before treatment can preserve semen for future fertility.</data>
      <data key="d8">SPERM BANKING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a type of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy may be used in addition to chemotherapy for stage IV anal cancer that has not had prior radiation</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A health care provider gives information about next steps and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="TREATMENT PLANNING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment planning decides the treatment options for the patient.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team is central to the entire cancer treatment process, particularly for patients with acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Their responsibilities begin with planning the next steps of care, which are determined based on imaging and test results. Using these findings, the care team identifies the appropriate course of action and outlines the treatment pathway. The care team provides treatment options and comprehensive care to the patient. Treatment planning is a collaborative process: the patient and care team together choose a treatment plan that aligns with the patient's needs and preferences. Once a plan is agreed upon, the care team coordinates care throughout the entire treatment journey, ensuring that all aspects of the plan are implemented smoothly. In addition to planning, the care team actively prescribes and monitors treatments. They ensure that each treatment is properly initiated, and they list the start date of each treatment. This monitoring is essential for tracking the patient's response and adjusting the plan as needed. Ultimately, the care team provides the planned cancer treatment itself, delivering the therapies that have been selected for the patient. Through this combination of planning, prescribing, monitoring, coordinating, and providing care, the care team manages the full continuum of AML treatment, supporting the patient from initial decision-making through ongoing therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient may avoid or delay treatment if biomarker testing shows lower-risk cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cancer treatment is a specific type of treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="HEARING TEST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment of head and neck tumors can affect hearing, so a hearing test may be used.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery is a type of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a form of medical treatment used primarily in oncology. As a cancer treatment, chemotherapy involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It is applicable across multiple cancer types and is often integrated into broader therapeutic protocols. Specifically, chemotherapy is a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it serves as the standard-of-care approach for managing this blood cancer. In addition to its role in AML, chemotherapy is also a component of cervical cancer treatment, frequently administered as part of chemoradiationa combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy that enhances the effectiveness of treatment. As such, chemotherapy occupies a central position within the overall category of cancer treatment, functioning both as a standalone modality and as an adjunct to other therapies. The entity TREATMENT encompasses a wide range of medical interventions, and chemotherapy is one of its key subtypes, distinguished by its mechanism of action and its established role in hematologic and solid tumor malignancies. Overall, chemotherapy exemplifies a targeted and evidence-based treatment option, with particular significance in AML, where it is the established standard of care, and in cervical cancer, where it is integrated into combined-modality regimens.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a type of treatment that falls under the broader category of medical treatments. Specifically, it is a type of cancer treatment, used to address malignant diseases. Additionally, radiation therapy is a type of treatment for cervical cancer, indicating its application in managing that particular form of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment is evaluated in metastatic cancer, and targeted therapy may be used for certain metastatic cases.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment is planned and given to manage prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="DOCTOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The doctor explains medical information, including treatment plans, and should be asked for simpler terms when needed.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Systemic therapy is a major type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML). As a treatment approach, systemic therapy works throughout the body, distinguishing it from localized treatments. It is specifically recognized as a type of AML treatment, meaning it is used to address the disease on a systemic level. Systemic therapy is therefore an important component of the broader treatment landscape for AML, offering a method that reaches cancer cells regardless of their location in the body.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Primary CNS lymphoma can be treated to slow growth, reduce symptoms, and prolong life.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="BLADDER CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment is provided for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="RECURRENCE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment and recurrence are closely linked concepts in cancer care. After a patient completes treatment, follow-up imaging is commonly used to monitor for any signs that the cancer has returned, a process known as recurrence. This surveillance helps detect whether the cancer has come back after initial therapy. Additionally, risk assessment plays a key role in evaluating the likelihood of recurrence following treatment, as well as determining whether a recurrence can be effectively managed with an alternative treatment approach. Together, these descriptions highlight that treatment is not only aimed at eliminating cancer but also involves ongoing monitoring and risk evaluation to address the possibility of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials are a type of treatment option, specifically one that may be recommended for patients with acute myeloid leukemia (AML). As a treatment approach, clinical trials offer eligible individuals access to investigational therapies and contribute to the advancement of medical knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="SIDE EFFECTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatments can cause side effects and side effects are a key part of treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d8">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="SUPPORTIVE CARE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supportive care is a type of treatment and care option, particularly used for acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Its primary purpose is to help manage the side effects of the disease and its treatment, aiming to improve the patient's quality of life and overall well-being. As a form of treatment, supportive care works alongside other medical interventions to address complications, reduce symptoms, and support the patient throughout their care journey.</data>
      <data key="d8">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CURE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment can sometimes lead to a cure, especially if started early</data>
      <data key="d8">CURE</data>
    </edge>
