      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Patients are told to ask their treatment team whether they meet the requirements for a clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="NCCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN provides a clinical trial finding resource at NCCN.org/cancercenters.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The clinical trials process is closely coordinated with the patient's care team. Patients are advised to ask their care team about access to clinical trials, and the care team plays a central role in facilitating clinical trial participation. In this capacity, the care team also advises the patient on whether a clinical trial might be right for them, helping to guide the decision-making process. Together, these responsibilities ensure that patients receive personalized support and clear information when considering clinical trial options.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials are listed as a treatment option for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="STUDY SPONSOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The study sponsor pays for research-related costs, including the study drug, so there is no fee to enroll.</data>
      <data key="d8">STUDY SPONSOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="RESEARCH TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials are research studies conducted to evaluate medical, surgical, or behavioral interventions. These trials are managed by a dedicated research team, which plays a central role in the study process. The research team is responsible for reviewing the study details with participants, ensuring they understand the purpose, procedures, risks, and potential benefits before enrollment. In addition to guiding participants through the informed consent process, the research team actively helps collect research data throughout the trial. They also coordinate patient care, working alongside healthcare providers to ensure that participants receive appropriate medical attention and that the trial protocol is followed. Thus, the research team bridges the gap between clinical care and research, supporting both data integrity and participant well-being. Together, clinical trials and the research team form an integrated framework for advancing medical knowledge while maintaining patient safety and care coordination.</data>
      <data key="d8">RESEARCH TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NLM operates clinicaltrials.gov, a database for finding clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="FDA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials give people access to investigational treatments that may, in time, be approved by the FDA. FDA may approve a treatment after Phase 3 clinical trial results are good, and Phase 4 trials study safety of FDA-approved treatments.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="PHASE 3 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIALS are research studies conducted to evaluate medical, surgical, or behavioral interventions. PHASE 3 TRIALS are a specific stage within the clinical trial process. According to the provided descriptions, phase 3 trials are a phase of clinical trials, and they are also identified as one phase of cancer clinical trials. This means that phase 3 trials serve as a key component of the broader clinical research framework, particularly in the context of cancer treatment development. They represent a later stage of testing, typically involving larger groups of participants to confirm the effectiveness of a treatment, monitor side effects, and compare it with standard treatments. As a phase of both general clinical trials and cancer-specific clinical trials, phase 3 studies are essential for generating rigorous evidence about new interventions before they may be considered for widespread use. Overall, phase 3 trials are a recognized and important phase within clinical trials, including those focused on cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 3 TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials are presented as a possible treatment option for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN Cancer Centers are listed as a resource for finding clinical trials in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials study the safety and effectiveness of investigational cancer treatments. Clinical trials may be included as options within a cancer treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Systemic therapy and clinical trials are both treatment options for squamous cell skin cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="PHASE 1 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase 1 trials are a specific phase within the broader framework of clinical trials. Clinical trials are research studies conducted to evaluate medical, surgical, or behavioral interventions, and they are typically divided into several sequential phases. Phase 1 trials represent the earliest stage of testing in humans, focusing primarily on safety, dosage, and how the treatment is processed by the body. These trials are also a key phase within cancer clinical trials specifically, where they are used to assess new oncology treatments. In the context of cancer, Phase 1 trials help determine the maximum tolerated dose, identify side effects, and gather preliminary evidence of effectiveness. Thus, Phase 1 trials serve as an essential component of both general clinical research and the specialized field of cancer clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 1 TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The treatment team considers many factors to decide if a stem cell rescue is right for a patient</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="SUPPORTIVE CARE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supportive care helps manage side effects and symptoms that arise from stem cell rescue and its high-dose chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stem cell rescue may be used for primary CNS lymphoma after other treatments or relapse.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="RELAPSE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stem cell rescue is a treatment approach used for lymphoma when the disease has returned after an initial course of therapy. Specifically, it is indicated in the context of a relapse, meaning the lymphoma has come back following earlier treatment. The procedure is employed after the relapse occurs, serving as a subsequent line of intervention. Thus, stem cell rescue is directly associated with managing recurrent lymphoma, with the goal of addressing the disease after prior treatment has failed to provide a lasting remission.</data>
      <data key="d8">RELAPSE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stem cell rescue is used when lymphoma comes back after earlier treatment or after other treatments</data>
      <data key="d8">LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scan obtains pictures of the cancer sites</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="IMAGING TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scan is a type of imaging test</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scan is an imaging test used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A CT scan (computed tomography) is a medical imaging technique that can reveal suspicious areas within the body. When such an area is identified, a biopsy may be needed to sample the tissue for diagnosis. In this context, CT can also be used to guide the biopsy procedure, helping clinicians precisely target the suspicious region. Together, CT scanning and biopsy are complementary: the CT scan locates and characterizes the area of concern, while the biopsy, often guided by CT, obtains a tissue sample for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="LYMPH NODE BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scan may be used to guide a lymph node biopsy</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scan is a type of imaging procedure.</data>
      <data key="d8">CT SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="PET-CT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT is often combined with PET to create a PET/CT scan.</data>
      <data key="d8">CT SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Endorectal ultrasound may be done instead of pelvic MRI when MRI cannot be used or its images are unhelpful</data>
      <data key="d8">ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MRI is an imaging test used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="BRAIN MRI" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brain MRI is an MRI application for the brain</data>
      <data key="d8">BRAIN MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="SPINE MRI" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Spine MRI is an MRI application for the spine</data>
      <data key="d8">SPINE MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) can play a key role in biopsy procedures. It is capable of revealing suspicious areas within the body that may require sampling by biopsy. Additionally, MRI can be used to guide a biopsy, helping to ensure that the procedure targets the identified area accurately. Thus, MRI serves both as a detection tool and as a guidance method for biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Transvaginal ultrasound may be used when MRI of the pelvis cannot be done.</data>
      <data key="d8">TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="ULTRASOUND" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MRI and ultrasound are imaging tests used in breast cancer evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MRI is a type of imaging test.</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="ULTRASOUND IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ultrasound imaging can be combined with MRI for accurate pictures.</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FERTILITY PRESERVATION" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Patients are advised to ask their care team about fertility changes and referrals to fertility specialists for preservation options before treatment.</data>
