      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="CREATE A MEDICAL BINDER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A medical binder helps organize records about a patient's specific type of head and neck cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">CREATE A MEDICAL BINDER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Head and neck cancer is a group of cancers that arise in the head or neck area.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="SPEECH AND SWALLOWING ASSESSMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Head and neck cancer treatment may affect swallowing and speech, so these abilities are tested before and after treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">SPEECH AND SWALLOWING ASSESSMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="TOBACCO AND ALCOHOL USE HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Tobacco and alcohol use are risk factors for head and neck cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">TOBACCO AND ALCOHOL USE HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a treatment for head and neck cancer that can affect fertility</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="THROAT CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Throat cancer is included in the group of cancers arising in the head and neck area.</data>
      <data key="d8">THROAT CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="HYPOPHARYNGEAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Head and neck cancer can occur in the hypopharynx, so hypopharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">HYPOPHARYNGEAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="OROPHARYNGEAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Oropharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer that arises in the oropharynx, which is part of the throat. Head and neck cancer is a broader category of malignancies, and oropharyngeal cancer represents a specific subtype originating in the oropharynx.</data>
      <data key="d8">OROPHARYNGEAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a treatment for head and neck cancer that can affect endocrine glands and hearing.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Head and neck cancer and its treatment can affect the jaw.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="PHYSICAL EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The physical exam is part of evaluating head and neck cancer; it includes the head and neck.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Head and neck cancer is a broad category of cancers that affect regions including the oral cavity, throat, larynx, sinuses, and salivary glands. Nasopharyngeal cancer is a specific type of head and neck cancer that arises in the nasopharynx, which is the upper part of the throat located behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. As a subtype of head and neck cancer, nasopharyngeal cancer shares the general characteristics of this disease group but is distinguished by its anatomical origin. Both descriptions confirm that nasopharyngeal cancer originates specifically in the nasopharynx and falls under the classification of head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is closely associated with head and neck cancer. As the most common type of mouth cancer, and since mouth cancer is a type of head and neck cancer, SCC is a significant subtype within this broader category. Additionally, squamous cell carcinoma arising in the nasopharynx is also a form of head and neck cancer. Therefore, SCC can manifest in various locations within the head and neck region, including the oral cavity and nasopharynx, and is recognized as a major histological type of head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="FERTILITY PRESERVATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cancer treatment for head and neck cancer can affect fertility, so fertility preservation options should be discussed before starting treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="MOUTH CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Mouth cancer is a type of head and neck cancer, listed as cancer of the oral cavity.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="HEARING TEST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Tumors in the head and neck and their treatment can affect hearing, making hearing tests necessary.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="PERFORMANCE STATUS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Performance status is considered when choosing a treatment plan for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="PREGNANCY TEST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Those with head and neck cancer who can become pregnant will have a pregnancy test before starting treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="LARYNGEAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Laryngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer, arising in the larynx/voice box.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="NUTRITION ASSESSMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Patients should meet with a nutrition expert before starting treatment for head and neck cancer to ensure adequate nutrition.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="PREVENTING PREGNANCY DURING TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cancer and cancer treatment for head and neck cancer can affect ovaries and damage sperm, so preventing pregnancy during treatment is important.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="ENDOCRINE TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy to head and neck cancer can affect endocrine glands, so blood tests may be done before and/or after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="DENTAL EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Head and neck cancer treatment can significantly affect the jaw, teeth, and gums, making dental health a critical part of patient care. Because of these potential impacts, a dental exam is highly important for individuals undergoing or preparing for treatment. Regular dental cleanings and checkups are essential to monitor and maintain oral health, as head and neck cancer therapiessuch as radiation or surgerycan compromise the structures of the mouth and jaw. Dental exams help identify and manage complications early, reducing the risk of serious dental issues during and after cancer treatment. Thus, for head and neck cancer patients, consistent dental care is a vital component of overall treatment and long-term wellbeing.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="DENTIST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">It is important to tell your dentist about head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="DISTRESS SCREENING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Distress screening is part of cancer care for patients diagnosed with cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="NASAL CAVITY CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Head and neck cancer can occur in the nasal cavity, so nasal cavity cancer is a type of head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="PARANASAL SINUS CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Head and neck cancer can occur in the paranasal sinuses, so paranasal sinus cancer is a type of head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A treatment team works with the patient to choose the best treatments for advanced-stage prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER" target="TUMOR STAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Tumor staging identifies whether prostate cancer has reached the advanced stage</data>
      <data key="d8">TUMOR STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Advanced-stage prostate cancer is defined by metastasis, meaning the cancer has spread beyond the prostate to other parts of the body. This condition is characterized by the movement of cancerous cells from the prostate gland to distant sites, such as bones, lymph nodes, or other organs. In the context of this disease, metastasis is the key feature that distinguishes advanced-stage prostate cancer from localized prostate cancer, which remains confined within the prostate. Thus, advanced-stage prostate cancer and metastasis are closely linked, with the latter serving as the defining criterion for the former.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LAB TESTING" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The treatment team uses lab testing information to plan treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LAB TESTING" target="NEUROSURGEON" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Lab testing results are sent back to the neurosurgeon.</data>
      <data key="d8">NEUROSURGEON</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LAB TESTING" target="NEUROPATHOLOGIST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The neuropathologist performs the laboratory analysis of biopsy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d8">NEUROPATHOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The radiologist writes an imaging report following an examination and sends it to the patient's health care provider. This report is a formal documentation of the radiologist's findings and interpretations. The health care provider receives this imaging report to inform patient care decisions. Thus, the radiologist and the health care provider are connected through the delivery of the written imaging report, ensuring that diagnostic information reaches the provider responsible for the patient's treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="ULTRASOUND" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A radiologist is a medical professional who specializes in interpreting imaging tests, including ultrasound. In this role, the radiologist reads and interprets ultrasound images to assist in diagnosing and monitoring medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOLECULAR TESTING" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Molecular testing results help the treatment team identify tumor type and growth rate to plan treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOLECULAR TESTING" target="NEUROPATHOLOGIST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The neuropathologist performs molecular testing on the biopsy sample.</data>
      <data key="d8">NEUROPATHOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOLECULAR TESTING" target="NEUROSURGEON" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Molecular testing results help guide the neurosurgeon and treatment team in managing the glioma.</data>
      <data key="d8">NEUROSURGEON</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOLECULAR TESTING" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Molecular testing is performed on a biopsy sample to identify tumor characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d8">MOLECULAR TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NUTRITION ASSESSMENT" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The treatment team monitors the patient for weight loss and other signs of inadequate nutrition during and after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NUTRITION ASSESSMENT" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Nutrition assessment is recommended before starting treatment and is monitored during and after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">NUTRITION ASSESSMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="LYNCH SYNDROME" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing can identify Lynch syndrome and other hereditary syndromes</data>
      <data key="d8">LYNCH SYNDROME</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="HEREDITARY OVARIAN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing can identify inherited mutations that cause hereditary ovarian cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">HEREDITARY OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="MUTATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing is done to look for inherited and tumor-related mutations</data>
      <data key="d8">MUTATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="FAMILY HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Family history of cancer may make genetic testing important</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="HEREDITARY GENE TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hereditary gene testing is one of the two basic types of genetic testing for prostate cancer care</data>
      <data key="d8">HEREDITARY GENE TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="TUMOR TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Tumor testing is one of the two basic types of genetic testing for prostate cancer care</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="GERMLINE TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing is a broad term that encompasses various methods used to analyze an individual's DNA. Germline testing is a specific type of genetic testing that focuses on identifying inherited mutations. These mutations are present from birth and can be passed down from parents to their children. By looking for these inherited changes, germline testing helps assess an individual's risk for certain genetic conditions and can inform medical decisions.</data>
      <data key="d8">GERMLINE TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="GENETIC COUNSELOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing may be done through a genetic counselor, who helps patients understand the results</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC COUNSELOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing may be done through the gynecology or oncology care team</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="DOCTOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Doctors use health history, family history, and genetic testing to assess hereditary ovarian cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">In tumor testing, a type of genetic testing, a biopsy sample is used for analysis</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
