      <data key="d7">Egg freezing may be discussed to preserve fertility in cervical cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d8">EGG FREEZING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ovarian transposition may be an option for cervical cancer patients receiving EBRT</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of cervical cancer. It arises from the squamous epithelial cells that line the outer surface of the cervix, and the vast majority of cervical cancers are of this subtype. Early detection through regular screening and HPV vaccination plays a key role in reducing the incidence and mortality of both squamous cell carcinoma and cervical cancer overall.</data>
      <data key="d8">SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="ADENOCARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Adenocarcinoma is a type of cervical cancer that forms in glandular cells.</data>
      <data key="d8">ADENOCARCINOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="ADENOSQUAMOUS CARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Adenosquamous carcinoma is a less common type of cervical cancer containing both squamous and adenocarcinoma cells.</data>
      <data key="d8">ADENOSQUAMOUS CARCINOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastasis is the spread of cervical cancer to distant sites, corresponding to stage 4B.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="CANCER SPREAD" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer can spread, generally first to the pelvic lymph nodes. Cancer spread describes the extent or dissemination of cervical cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER SPREAD</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET SCAN" target="INFECTION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Infection can show up as a bright spot on a PET scan</data>
      <data key="d8">INFECTION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET SCAN" target="CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">PET scan shows cancer cells because they use sugar faster than normal cells</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET SCAN" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">PET imaging can show how well treatment is working.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
