    </edge>
    <edge source="TESTS" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team may order or consider possible tests for the patient</data>
      <data key="d8">TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="INSURANCE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During a clinical trial, the patient's standard cancer care is billed to insurance.</data>
      <data key="d8">INSURANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A clinical trial is a treatment option that may be considered for a patient. Clinical trials offer potential new therapies and serve as another treatment option.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="NCCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN Cancer Centers and NCCN patient guidelines provide information about cancer clinical trials and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Enrolling in a clinical trial is another treatment option alongside surgery for eligible patients.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team plays a key role in evaluating whether a clinical trial is appropriate for a patient. They can be consulted directly to determine if a clinical trial might be right for the individual. To make this determination, the care team uses the patient's performance status as a key factor in assessing eligibility for a clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="UNITED STATES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text describes finding clinical trials in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCI provides a portal for patients to search for cancer clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NLM maintains clinicaltrials.gov, where patients can search for clinical trials worldwide.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="STUDY SPONSOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The study sponsor pays for research-related costs of the clinical trial, including the study drug.</data>
      <data key="d8">STUDY SPONSOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 3 CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Phase 3 trial is a clinical trial phase that compares a new treatment to a standard treatment and may lead to FDA approval.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 3 CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 3 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase 3 trials are a phase of clinical trials comparing a new approach to a standard treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 3 TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Joining a clinical trial is a recommended option for treating primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="INDUCTION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trial is one of the induction treatment options discussed for primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">INDUCTION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The NCCN Cancer Centers serve as a key resource for locating clinical trials, specifically within the United States. These centers assist patients in identifying and accessing clinical trial opportunities, making them a valuable source of information for those seeking participation in research studies. The entity CLINICAL TRIAL is central to this resource, as the primary function of the NCCN Cancer Centers is to connect individuals with relevant clinical trials. In summary, NCCN Cancer Centers provide a United Statesbased resource dedicated to helping patients find clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 2 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase 2 trials are a phase of clinical trials studying effectiveness against a specific type of cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 4 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase 4 trials are a phase of clinical trials studying long-term safety and benefit after FDA approval</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 1 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase 1 trials are a phase of clinical trials studying safety and side effects</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 1 TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase 1 trials are an early phase of clinical trials</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 1 TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 1 CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Phase 1 trial is an early phase in the clinical trial process, focused on dose, safety, and side effects.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 1 CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="TREATMENT TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials are research studies that evaluate medical interventions in human participants. Treatment trials are a specific type of clinical trial, meaning that all treatment trials fall under the broader category of clinical trials. In the context of cancer, treatment trials focus specifically on cancer treatmentstesting new therapies, drug combinations, surgical techniques, or other interventions to determine their safety and effectiveness. Because most cancer clinical trials are treatment trials, treatment trials constitute a major subset of clinical research in oncology. Thus, while every treatment trial is a clinical trial, not every clinical trial is a treatment trial; clinical trials also include prevention, screening, diagnostic, and quality-of-life studies. In summary, treatment trials are a type of clinical trial that centers on cancer treatments and represents the majority of cancer-related clinical research.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 4 CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Phase 4 trial is a post-approval clinical trial phase that studies long-term safety and benefit.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 2" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase 2 is a later phase of clinical trials, mentioned immediately after Phase 1.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 2 CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Phase 2 trial is a clinical trial phase that studies how well a drug works against a specific type of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Enrolling in a clinical trial is encouraged for metastatic anal cancer if available.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIAGNOSIS" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Diagnosis and treatment are closely connected in prostate cancer care. A diagnosis of prostate cancer risk is used to guide treatment decisions, helping the care team determine which treatment is best for the patient. After the diagnosis is confirmed, the care team discusses the available treatment options with the patient, ensuring that the chosen approach aligns with the individual's risk level and overall situation.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIAGNOSIS" target="TREATMENT PLANNING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Diagnosis determines the treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT PLANNING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIAGNOSIS" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient receives a diagnosis from biopsy results.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIAGNOSIS" target="PROGNOSIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Diagnosis identifies the illness, while prognosis predicts the likely course and outcome; they are distinct but related concepts</data>
      <data key="d8">PROGNOSIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIAGNOSIS" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Primary CNS lymphoma requires several tests to confirm the diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIAGNOSIS" target="EYE EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Eye exam helps diagnose primary CNS lymphoma if the eyes are involved</data>
      <data key="d8">EYE EXAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIAGNOSIS" target="NEUROLOGICAL EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A neurological exam is used in the diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d8">NEUROLOGICAL EXAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIAGNOSIS" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biopsy is key to confirming the diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic disease affects treatment planning and may be classified as very advanced disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Anal cancer can metastasize through lymphatics or blood to distant areas</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="TUMOR STAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Tumor staging uses the M category to indicate whether cancer has metastasized outside the pelvis</data>
      <data key="d8">TUMOR STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="PALLIATIVE RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If metastatic cancer causes symptoms, palliative radiation therapy may be used to limit tumor size and help with symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d8">PALLIATIVE RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="DCF" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">DCF is used to treat metastatic anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">DCF</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="FOLFCIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">FOLFCIS is used to treat metastatic anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">FOLFCIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="METASTATIC BREAST CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic breast cancer results when breast cancer cells spread through metastasis to distant sites in the body.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="FIRST-LINE THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic anal cancer is treated first with platinum-based chemotherapy, called first-line therapy</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If cancer metastasizes after initial radiation therapy, radiation therapy cannot be used again on the same areas.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Prostate cancer can spread to other areas of the body through metastasis, and this spread is called advanced-stage disease</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="CANCER STAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The presence or absence of metastasis is a key factor in determining cancer stage and is represented in the M score.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Advanced-stage prostate cancer involves spread of cancer cells; this spreading is called metastasis</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="FOLFOX" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">FOLFOX is used to treat metastatic anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="PROGRESSION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic cancer may progress during or after chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="METASTATIC PROSTATE CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic prostate cancer has spread through metastasis; the text uses the terms interchangeably</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="PERINEURAL INVASION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Perineural invasion is considered high- or very high-risk, indicating an increased risk of spread.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLOOD TESTS" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Blood tests are used to check for signs of disease and how well organs are working during treatment planning and monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLOOD TESTS" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient should obtain copies of their blood tests and file them with their medical records.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLOOD TESTS" target="COLON CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Blood tests are recommended as part of initial testing for colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLOOD TESTS" target="BLOOD UREA NITROGEN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">BUN is measured in a blood test.</data>
      <data key="d8">BLOOD UREA NITROGEN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLOOD TESTS" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Blood tests are included in the workup for primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLOOD TESTS" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Blood tests are part of the workup for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLOOD TESTS" target="COMPLETE BLOOD COUNT AND DIFFERENTIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CBC and differential are blood tests</data>
      <data key="d8">COMPLETE BLOOD COUNT AND DIFFERENTIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is a type of treatment for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is a treatment modality that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. Specifically, chemoradiation includes chemotherapy administered concurrently with radiation therapy, meaning the drug component is given at the same time as the radiation treatment. In the context of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), chemotherapy serves as the drug component delivered concurrently with EBRT as part of the chemoradiation regimen. The available descriptions indicate that chemoradiation, chemotherapy, or both may be listed as possible next treatment options for a patient, depending on the clinical scenario. Chemotherapy, as the drug component, works in conjunction with radiation to enhance the overall treatment effect. Thus, chemoradiation represents the integration of systemic drug therapy and localized radiation, while chemotherapy itself can also be considered independently as a treatment option.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is the recommended primary treatment for non-metastatic anal canal cancer and is used for anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="ANAL CANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is the recommended primary treatment for non-metastatic anal canal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="APR SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">APR surgery may be needed when anal cancer does not respond to initial treatment such as chemoradiation or returns after it</data>
      <data key="d8">APR SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="MITOMYCIN AND CAPECITABINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">This regimen is one of the chemotherapy regimens used as part of chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d8">MITOMYCIN AND CAPECITABINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="HEALTH CARE PROFESSIONALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Health care professionals describe prostate cancer aggressiveness, recommend screening, and treat the disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROFESSIONALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="PEOPLE WITH INHERITED GENETIC MUTATIONS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">People with certain inherited genetic mutations are at greater risk for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">PEOPLE WITH INHERITED GENETIC MUTATIONS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="EVERYONE WITH A PROSTATE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Everyone with a prostate is at risk for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">EVERYONE WITH A PROSTATE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="GLEASON SCORE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Gleason score estimates how aggressive prostate cancer is and whether it will spread.</data>
      <data key="d8">GLEASON SCORE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biopsy samples are examined to determine whether prostate cancer is present.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="RESEARCHERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Researchers are trying to understand the reasons for prostate cancer disparities and improve outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d8">RESEARCHERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Prostate cancer can advance by metastasis, a process defined as the spread of cancer cells outside the prostate to other organs and tissues. This form of progression is characteristic of advanced-stage prostate cancer. Metastasis occurs when cancer cells travel through the bloodstream or the lymphatic system, enabling the cancer to disseminate beyond the prostate to distant sites in the body. In this way, prostate cancer metastasis involves the movement of malignant cells from the prostate via circulatory or lymphatic routes, ultimately establishing secondary growths in other organs and tissues.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Systemic therapy is a type of treatment for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Enrolling in a clinical trial for systemic therapy is an option.</data>
      <data key="d8">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Systemic therapy and clinical trials are both treatment options for squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a specific type of systemic therapy. As a systemic therapy, chemotherapy is administered intravenously and travels through the bloodstream, allowing it to reach cells throughout the body rather than targeting only one localized area. This broad distribution is a defining characteristic of systemic treatment. Chemotherapy is also one of the most common types of systemic therapy used for bladder cancer. Thus, while systemic therapy is the broader category encompassing various treatment approaches that affect the whole body, chemotherapy is a prominent and widely utilized form of that category, particularly in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="FERTILITY PRESERVATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Systemic therapy can affect fertility, so patients are advised to discuss fertility preservation with their care team before therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY PRESERVATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Systemic therapy is a type of cancer treatment using medicines that travel through the bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Systemic therapy may be given first for advanced cancers or cancer that has spread to lymph nodes; metastasis indicates advanced/spread cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="BIOMARKER TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biomarker testing plays a critical role in the management of recurrent cancers, helping to guide treatment decisions and identify potential therapeutic options. In the context of recurrent ovarian cancer, biomarker testing is used to help direct treatment by providing information that can inform which therapies are most likely to be effective for the individual patient. For recurrent cervical cancer, biomarker testing is specifically recommended for all patients with disease recurrence, as it serves to identify targeted therapy options or determine eligibility for clinical trials. Thus, across both recurrent ovarian and recurrent cervical cancers, biomarker testing is an essential tool for personalizing care after a recurrence, enabling clinicians to match patients with the most appropriate treatments, including targeted therapies, and to explore clinical trial opportunities when standard options are limited.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Anal cancer may return after a cancer-free period, which is called recurrence</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="PHYSICAL EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A physical exam may first raise suspicion of recurrence because of symptoms or abnormal findings.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHYSICAL EXAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If cancer returns to the anal area, abdominoperineal resection is recommended</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Patients with persistent or recurrent ovarian cancer are encouraged to consider a clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Recurrence can include metastasis of the cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FERTILITY AND FAMILY PLANNING" target="COLON CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Young adults diagnosed with colon cancer should be counseled about fertility risks and preservation options.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FERTILITY AND FAMILY PLANNING" target="OVARIAN TISSUE BANKING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ovarian tissue banking is a fertility preservation option related to fertility and family planning counseling.</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY AND FAMILY PLANNING</data>
    </edge>
