    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="REGULAR SCREENING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Regular screening helps find breast cancer early</data>
      <data key="d8">REGULAR SCREENING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="EARLY DETECTION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Early detection aims to find breast cancer earlier and reduce the risk of dying from it</data>
      <data key="d8">EARLY DETECTION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="GENETIC CANCER RISK TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic cancer risk testing evaluates a person's risk of developing breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC CANCER RISK TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="DUCTAL CARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ductal carcinoma is the most common type of breast cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="INVASIVE BREAST CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">BREAST CANCER encompasses various forms of malignancy originating in the breast. INVASIVE BREAST CANCER is a specific form of breast cancer. It is characterized by cancer that has spread from the ducts or lobules into the surrounding breast tissue or nearby lymph nodes. In other words, invasive breast cancer is a category of breast cancer in which malignant cells have broken out of their original sites within the milk ducts or lobules and invaded the adjacent breast tissue, with the potential to reach nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="BREAST CARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Breast carcinoma is a carcinoma of the breast and is closely related to breast cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) is a form of breast cancer, specifically classified as a noninvasive, stage 0 condition. As a breast cancer subtype, DCIS is characterized by the presence of abnormal cells that are confined within the milk ducts of the breast and have not spread into surrounding breast tissue. This noninvasive nature distinguishes it from invasive breast cancers, and it is considered the earliest stage of breast cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Breast cancer can metastasize, meaning cancer cells can spread through blood or lymph vessels to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="PREGNANCY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pregnancy is a condition considered in breast cancer screening and testing discussions</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="TESTING FOR BREAST CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Testing for breast cancer is performed to gather information about the cancer and determine a treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="LOBULAR CARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Lobular carcinoma is a type of breast cancer that starts in the lobules</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="CARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Almost all breast cancers are carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biopsy is used to obtain tissue for diagnosing breast cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery can be part of breast cancer diagnosis or treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
