    </edge>
    <edge source="TESTING FOR OVARIAN CANCER" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Testing provides information that informs the cancer treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d8">TESTING FOR OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLOOD THINNERS" target="DEEP VEIN THROMBOSIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Blood thinners are used after surgery to help prevent deep vein thrombosis</data>
      <data key="d8">DEEP VEIN THROMBOSIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLOOD THINNERS" target="HYSTERECTOMY AND BSO" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Many patients are placed on blood thinners for up to 4 weeks after hysterectomy and BSO to help prevent blood clots</data>
      <data key="d8">HYSTERECTOMY AND BSO</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="COLORECTAL CANCER SCREENING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Colorectal cancer screening can detect colon cancer early, when it is most treatable</data>
      <data key="d8">COLORECTAL CANCER SCREENING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an inherited syndrome closely related to colon cancer. In classic FAP, the polyps characteristic of the condition can become invasive colon cancer, often by age 50. Thus, colon cancer is a major complication of this inherited syndrome, with FAP serving as a direct precursor condition.</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Microsatellite instability testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with colon cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="LYNCH SYNDROME" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Lynch syndrome is an inherited syndrome directly related to colon cancer. Individuals born with Lynch syndrome face a significantly elevated risk of developing colon cancer, making it a key hereditary factor in the disease's onset.</data>
      <data key="d8">LYNCH SYNDROME</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">All colon cancers should be tested for mismatch repair mutations; mismatch repair testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with colon cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="POLYPS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Almost all colon cancers start in a polyp, and removing polyps can prevent colon cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="SURVEILLANCE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surveillance is part of the post-treatment management for colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="METASTATIC CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic cancer is a more advanced form of colon cancer that has spread</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="COLECTOMY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Colectomy is needed for colon cancer not found early enough to be removed by polypectomy; it removes the cancerous part of the colon.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="ADENOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Adenomas can become invasive colon cancer over time</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="COLONOSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Colon cancer often forms in polyps, which are abnormal growths on the inner lining of the colon. Colonoscopy is the medical procedure used to view and remove these polyps, and it plays a central role in both the detection and diagnosis of colon cancer. Most colon cancers are found during a colonoscopy, as the procedure allows direct visualization of the colon and the ability to biopsy or remove suspicious tissue. In some cases, another colonoscopy may be needed to confirm a diagnosis, particularly when initial findings require further evaluation or when polyp removal was incomplete. Thus, colonoscopy serves as both a screening and diagnostic tool for colon cancer, linking polyp detection and removal to the broader process of diagnosing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="NON-METASTATIC CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Non-metastatic cancer is colon cancer that has not spread and is treated with surgery and sometimes chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="SURVIVORSHIP" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Survivorship focuses on long-term side effects and healthy habits after colon cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="COLORECTAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Colon cancer is a colorectal cancer that forms in the colon</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="CHEMOTHERAPY AFTER SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The cancer stage determined after surgery helps determine whether chemotherapy after surgery is needed.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYPS" target="COLORECTAL CANCER SCREENING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Screening can find polyps so they can be removed before they become cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">COLORECTAL CANCER SCREENING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYPS" target="FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">FAP causes hundreds to thousands of polyps to form in the colon and rectum</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYPS" target="ADENOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Adenoma is the most common type of polyp.</data>
      <data key="d8">ADENOMA</data>
    </edge>
