    <edge source="DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR" target="MSI-H" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MSI-H is often due to deficient mismatch repair</data>
      <data key="d8">MSI-H</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY" target="BIOMARKER TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MSI is tested as a biomarker in recurrent ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY" target="MSI-H" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MSI-H stands for microsatellite instability-high</data>
      <data key="d8">MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY" target="DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Microsatellite instability (MSI) is a condition characterized by the accumulation of genetic alterations in microsatellite regionsshort, repetitive DNA sequences prone to errors during replication. Deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) is a key underlying cause of this instability. Specifically, MSI is often due to defects in the mismatch repair system, which normally corrects errors that occur during DNA replication. When the genes responsible for mismatch repair are deficient, replication errors in microsatellites are not corrected, leading to the high mutation rate and variability observed in these regions. Thus, microsatellite instability is frequently attributed to deficient mismatch repair genes, highlighting the direct relationship between the two entities: dMMR drives MSI, and MSI serves as a molecular indicator of defective mismatch repair function. This connection is important in contexts such as cancer diagnosis and treatment, where MSI/dMMR status can influence prognosis and therapeutic decisions. Overall, microsatellite instability often arises from deficient mismatch repair, and the two terms are closely linked in clinical and biological understanding.</data>
      <data key="d8">MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY (MSI)" target="MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MSI-H is high microsatellite instability, often due to dMMR genes, and is part of the MSI-H/dMMR tumor mutation status.</data>
      <data key="d8">MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY (MSI)" target="DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR (DMMR)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Deficient mismatch repair prevents DNA errors from being fixed, leading to microsatellite instability.</data>
      <data key="d8">DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR (DMMR)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surveillance is performed to find early signs that anal cancer may have returned</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="PROCTOSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
