    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="PHYSICAL EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Physical exams are part of surveillance for stage 2 and 3 colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHYSICAL EXAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="PHYSICAL AND PELVIC EXAMS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surveillance includes regular physical and pelvic exams.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHYSICAL AND PELVIC EXAMS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTATIC CANCER" target="BIOMARKER TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread from its original site to distant organs or tissues. In this context, biomarker testing plays a crucial role in guiding treatment decisions. For metastatic cancer, a tumor may be tested for specific biomarkers to help determine the most effective therapeutic approach. Biomarker testing involves analyzing tumor tissue or blood for genetic mutations, protein expression, or other molecular characteristics that can predict how a cancer will respond to certain treatments. This is particularly relevant for metastatic cervical cancer, where PD-L1 and biomarker testing is recommended. Testing for PD-L1 expression and other biomarkers in metastatic cervical cancer helps determine whether certain targeted therapies or immunotherapies, such as checkpoint inhibitors, may be viable options for the patient. By identifying the presence or absence of these biomarkers, oncologists can personalize treatment plans, potentially improving outcomes and avoiding ineffective therapies. Overall, biomarker testing in metastatic cancer, including cervical cancer, is an essential tool for precision medicine, enabling more tailored and effective treatment strategies based on the unique molecular profile of each patient's tumor.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTATIC CANCER" target="EXPANDED BIOMARKER TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Expanded biomarker testing is used to identify targetable features of metastatic cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTATIC CANCER" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer may spread to distant sites, referred to as metastatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTATIC CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTATIC CANCER" target="IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging such as CT, MRI, or PET/CT is used in metastatic cervical cancer to evaluate response to systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTATIC CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTATIC CANCER" target="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastatic cancer, specifically metastatic cervical cancer, is typically treated with systemic therapy, which is administered throughout the body to target cancer cells that have spread beyond the original site. Systemic therapy is a standard treatment approach for metastatic disease, and in the case of metastatic cervical cancer, it serves as the primary treatment modality. To evaluate the effectiveness of this treatment, imaging is used to monitor the cancer's response to systemic therapy. This monitoring helps clinicians assess whether the therapy is shrinking or controlling the tumors, and it guides ongoing treatment decisions. In summary, systemic therapy is the cornerstone of treatment for metastatic cancer, including metastatic cervical cancer, and imaging plays a crucial role in tracking treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTATIC CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTATIC CANCER" target="FIRST-LINE THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">First-line therapy is selected based on whether the metastatic cancer has the PD-L1 biomarker</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTATIC CANCER</data>
    </edge>
