    </edge>
    <edge source="OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION" target="OVARIES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ovarian transposition moves one or both ovaries out of the radiation beam range.</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION" target="OOPHOROPEXY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ovarian transposition is the surgery medically named oophoropexy; they are the same procedure.</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION" target="EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure performed prior to external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) to reposition one or both ovaries out of the radiation beam's range. The goal is to reduce radiation damage to the ovaries during EBRT, thereby preserving ovarian function. By moving the ovaries away from the targeted radiation field, the procedure minimizes the risk of radiation-induced ovarian failure. External beam radiation therapy, in turn, is the treatment modality that delivers targeted radiation to a specific area; ovarian transposition serves as a protective measure for patients undergoing this therapy. Together, the two entities represent a combined strategy to safeguard reproductive health while still benefiting from cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION" target="SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ovarian transposition may be an option for premenopausal women with squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both are resources that help patients find clinical trials; NLM provides clinicaltrials.gov and CIS offers direct assistance.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Cancer Information Service assists individuals in locating appropriate clinical trials, including providing guidance on how to find them. It also answers cancer-related questions and addresses specific inquiries about clinical trials, such as their purpose, procedures, and eligibility. Additionally, the service offers contact information for further assistance, ensuring that individuals have access to the support they need when seeking information about cancer and clinical trial participation.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LARYNGOSCOPY" target="DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Laryngoscopy is a medical procedure used to visualize the inside of the larynx (voice box) and nearby structures of the throat. Direct laryngoscopy is a specific type of laryngoscopy. In direct laryngoscopy, the practitioner inserts a rigid or flexible scope directly into the airway to obtain a clear view of the larynx, often for diagnostic or therapeutic purposes. Both descriptions confirm that direct laryngoscopy falls under the broader category of laryngoscopy, making it one of the methods within this diagnostic and interventional technique.</data>
      <data key="d8">LARYNGOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LARYNGOSCOPY" target="FIBEROPTIC LARYNGOSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Laryngoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the larynx (voice box) and surrounding structures. Fiberoptic laryngoscopy is a type of laryngoscopy that utilizes a flexible fiberoptic scope to visualize the larynx. In other words, fiberoptic laryngoscopy falls under the broader category of laryngoscopy, sharing the same diagnostic and interventional purpose but distinguished by its use of fiberoptic technology. Together, these terms describe a continuum of techniques for viewing the throat and airway, with fiberoptic laryngoscopy representing a specific, commonly used variation of the standard laryngoscopic approach.</data>
      <data key="d8">LARYNGOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LARYNGOSCOPY" target="PANENDOSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Panendoscopy can include laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d8">LARYNGOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
