    <edge source="STEPHANIE E. WEISS" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The MRI scan image credited to Stephanie E. Weiss highlights primary CNS lymphoma in the brain.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Mature B-cell ALL, also called Burkitt leukemia, is a rare subtype of B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d8">MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BURKITT LEUKEMIA" target="BURKITT LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Burkitt leukemia and Burkitt lymphoma are closely related conditions. Burkitt leukemia is essentially the same disease as Burkitt lymphoma, sharing the same underlying pathology. However, despite this fundamental similarity, the two are treated differently in clinical practice. The distinction reflects differences in how the disease presents and the treatment approach chosen, but the core disease process is considered the same entity.</data>
      <data key="d8">BURKITT LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NLPHL" target="DLBCL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NLPHL can transform over time into diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, a fast-growing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">DLBCL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TRANSLOCATION" target="EWING SARCOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ewing sarcoma is associated with translocations, including t(11;22).</data>
      <data key="d8">EWING SARCOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENE FUSION" target="EWING SARCOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ewing sarcoma is characterized by fusions of EWSR1 with ETS family genes such as FLI1.</data>
      <data key="d8">EWING SARCOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INDUCTION" target="TREATMENT FOR THE SPINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If cancer is found in the spine or spinal fluid, a different induction therapy may be recommended, linking treatment for the spine to the induction phase.</data>
      <data key="d8">INDUCTION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INDUCTION" target="CONSOLIDATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Induction is the first phase and consolidation is the second phase of treatment; consolidation follows induction and aims to destroy remaining cancer cells.</data>
